WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.320
&gt;&gt; All right.
Good morning everyone.

00:00:07.321 --> 00:00:08.490
Thank you for coming to

00:00:08.491 --> 00:00:10.315
the confidential
computing session.

00:00:10.316 --> 00:00:12.600
So, the session is

00:00:12.601 --> 00:00:15.065
all about security and
privacy for the Cloud.

00:00:15.066 --> 00:00:17.940
So, it's great to have you here,

00:00:17.941 --> 00:00:20.090
and we have a very,

00:00:20.091 --> 00:00:24.500
very exciting group of speakers.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:27.540
The list of achievements and

00:00:27.541 --> 00:00:29.440
the awards that they
have is so big that I

00:00:29.441 --> 00:00:33.610
had to print a page of paper
just to remind myself.

00:00:33.611 --> 00:00:35.850
So, I think it's going to
be an exciting session.

00:00:35.851 --> 00:00:38.945
The first speaker is
Mark Russinovich.

00:00:38.946 --> 00:00:42.710
Mark is the Chief Technology
Officer for Azure.

00:00:42.711 --> 00:00:46.600
He's an expert on Distributed
Systems, Operating Systems.

00:00:46.601 --> 00:00:50.900
He has the pleasure
and the responsibility

00:00:50.901 --> 00:00:53.350
of running one of
the largest Distributed Systems

00:00:53.351 --> 00:00:54.915
ever built by mankind.

00:00:54.916 --> 00:00:57.125
So, it's a tough job.

00:00:57.126 --> 00:00:59.950
Mark is also an author
of several books.

00:00:59.951 --> 00:01:02.355
He has ran startups.

00:01:02.356 --> 00:01:04.610
So, he has a very broad range of

00:01:04.611 --> 00:01:05.840
experience and he's going to

00:01:05.841 --> 00:01:07.400
tell us about Azure
confidential computing.

00:01:07.401 --> 00:01:08.390
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and we
found out yesterday

00:01:08.391 --> 00:01:10.240
Donald hasn't read my book.

00:01:10.241 --> 00:01:14.510
&gt;&gt; I was going to say I'm
putting it on my reading list.

00:01:14.511 --> 00:01:17.725
&gt;&gt; Okay, good.
Good morning everybody.

00:01:17.726 --> 00:01:19.100
So, this morning I'm
going to talk to you a

00:01:19.101 --> 00:01:20.400
little bit about what we call

00:01:20.401 --> 00:01:22.445
confidential computing
in our vision

00:01:22.446 --> 00:01:24.010
at Microsoft in Azure,

00:01:24.011 --> 00:01:25.970
specifically with MSR, on

00:01:25.971 --> 00:01:27.980
how this technology
is going to evolve,

00:01:27.981 --> 00:01:29.000
and eventually I think

00:01:29.001 --> 00:01:32.465
become standard for
all computation.

00:01:32.466 --> 00:01:34.100
I'll start by talking
about the way

00:01:34.101 --> 00:01:36.025
that we approached it in Azure,

00:01:36.026 --> 00:01:39.620
starting eight years ago
when I began in Azure.

00:01:39.621 --> 00:01:41.890
Even back then, people
were looking at

00:01:41.891 --> 00:01:44.300
the Cloud skeptically for
a lot of different reasons.

00:01:44.301 --> 00:01:45.340
But one of them of course,

00:01:45.341 --> 00:01:48.515
a big one was, the security
of their data in the Cloud.

00:01:48.516 --> 00:01:50.210
The various threats that they

00:01:50.211 --> 00:01:51.740
thought their data would
be exposed to if they

00:01:51.741 --> 00:01:53.585
stored their data in

00:01:53.586 --> 00:01:54.740
a Cloud provider where it's

00:01:54.741 --> 00:01:55.860
being managed by somebody else.

00:01:55.861 --> 00:01:58.460
Including, for example,
a hacker breaking in

00:01:58.461 --> 00:01:59.690
through the
multi-tenancy boundary

00:01:59.691 --> 00:02:01.485
and getting access to their data,

00:02:01.486 --> 00:02:06.890
malicious insider taking
their data and releasing it,

00:02:06.891 --> 00:02:09.980
or a third party coming and then,

00:02:09.981 --> 00:02:13.760
through legal means,
coercing or having

00:02:13.761 --> 00:02:16.390
the Cloud provider turnover
the customer's data,

00:02:16.391 --> 00:02:19.595
and all of these without
the customer's knowledge.

00:02:19.596 --> 00:02:22.385
All of these we wanted
to make sure at

00:02:22.386 --> 00:02:24.900
Microsoft that, as
much as possible,

00:02:24.901 --> 00:02:27.530
we could protect
the customer data

00:02:27.531 --> 00:02:29.900
in light of these threats,

00:02:29.901 --> 00:02:33.410
and specifically for
the legal request

00:02:33.411 --> 00:02:36.290
for customer data that we
wouldn't be put in the middle,

00:02:36.291 --> 00:02:37.850
that it would be a matter between

00:02:37.851 --> 00:02:39.945
law enforcement and the customer.

00:02:39.946 --> 00:02:43.040
So, we started looking at how we

00:02:43.041 --> 00:02:47.230
could increase the
protection of our data.

00:02:47.231 --> 00:02:49.400
If you take a look at the way
that Cloud providers have

00:02:49.401 --> 00:02:51.380
evolved the protection
that data, of course,

00:02:51.381 --> 00:02:53.975
one of the things we've
seen over time is that

00:02:53.976 --> 00:02:57.500
all of us have put in mechanisms
to encrypt data at rest,

00:02:57.501 --> 00:03:00.305
starting with system managed
keys and then adding

00:03:00.306 --> 00:03:02.060
customer managed keys or bring

00:03:02.061 --> 00:03:03.320
your own keys solutions

00:03:03.321 --> 00:03:04.995
for customers to
protect their data.

00:03:04.996 --> 00:03:07.760
We've always
encrypted our data in

00:03:07.761 --> 00:03:10.280
transit between data centers
and you're seeing us

00:03:10.281 --> 00:03:12.620
introduce encryption even if

00:03:12.621 --> 00:03:14.225
the customer's data links aren't

00:03:14.226 --> 00:03:16.510
encrypted within the data center.

00:03:16.511 --> 00:03:18.135
Then the missing piece though,

00:03:18.136 --> 00:03:19.730
was how do we protect

00:03:19.731 --> 00:03:22.060
the data while it's
being computed over.

00:03:22.061 --> 00:03:24.680
These mechanisms where customer

00:03:24.681 --> 00:03:26.840
would store their data
encrypted in the Cloud but then

00:03:26.841 --> 00:03:28.430
not be able to compute over it

00:03:28.431 --> 00:03:31.515
are fairly uninteresting to
the customers and to us.

00:03:31.516 --> 00:03:33.220
At that point the Cloud

00:03:33.221 --> 00:03:35.050
is just this giant
storage solution.

00:03:35.051 --> 00:03:36.590
The customer still left with

00:03:36.591 --> 00:03:38.120
providing computational resources

00:03:38.121 --> 00:03:39.770
outside the Cloud and having

00:03:39.771 --> 00:03:41.540
to worry about transmitting
data back and forth.

00:03:41.541 --> 00:03:43.100
If we could bring

00:03:43.101 --> 00:03:44.810
the computation to
the data center

00:03:44.811 --> 00:03:46.219
and still protect the data,

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:48.080
then we'd achieve protection of

00:03:48.081 --> 00:03:51.185
the data through all aspects
of its life-cycle.

00:03:51.186 --> 00:03:55.055
So, we saw the development

00:03:55.056 --> 00:03:57.680
of what are called Trusted
Execution Environment.

00:03:57.681 --> 00:03:59.030
I believe everybody
in the room here

00:03:59.031 --> 00:04:00.170
is probably familiar with them.

00:04:00.171 --> 00:04:02.215
So, I won't spend
too much time on them,

00:04:02.216 --> 00:04:03.440
other than to say that they've

00:04:03.441 --> 00:04:04.550
interesting characteristics

00:04:04.551 --> 00:04:07.105
with respect to the problems
that we're solving here,

00:04:07.106 --> 00:04:10.200
or the fact that
it's a black box.

00:04:10.201 --> 00:04:11.950
From outside the black box,

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:13.220
you can't see what's going on

00:04:13.221 --> 00:04:15.265
inside the black box, ideally.

00:04:15.266 --> 00:04:17.570
The black box can
attest to what code is

00:04:17.571 --> 00:04:20.325
inside the black box
in a secure way,

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:23.040
and then the black box can also

00:04:23.041 --> 00:04:26.140
seal secrets inside of
itself for a later access.

00:04:26.141 --> 00:04:30.650
The two examples of
enclaves that we've seen,

00:04:30.651 --> 00:04:32.570
that are in practical use

00:04:32.571 --> 00:04:34.840
for Cloud providers would be SGX,

00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:36.290
which is part of
Intel processors,

00:04:36.291 --> 00:04:37.510
and then Virtual Secure Mode,

00:04:37.511 --> 00:04:39.020
which is a technology

00:04:39.021 --> 00:04:41.685
that was released in
Windows Server 2016.

00:04:41.686 --> 00:04:44.870
Unlike SGX, which is hardware
protection of the data,

00:04:44.871 --> 00:04:47.810
Virtual Secure Mode is hypervisor
protection of the data.

00:04:47.811 --> 00:04:49.620
So, even the host partition,

00:04:49.621 --> 00:04:52.325
a privileged partition,
a system administrator,

00:04:52.326 --> 00:04:55.370
wouldn't have access
to the data being

00:04:55.371 --> 00:04:58.805
protected by the Virtual
Secure Mode black box.

00:04:58.806 --> 00:05:05.300
The Trusted Executioner
application architecture

00:05:05.301 --> 00:05:08.119
that we see resulting
from these technologies,

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:11.124
where the enclave
process isolation,

00:05:11.125 --> 00:05:12.520
sub-process isolation,

00:05:12.521 --> 00:05:14.270
the black box
actually lives within

00:05:14.271 --> 00:05:17.120
a process in
the operating system,

00:05:17.121 --> 00:05:19.745
shares the address
space of that process.

00:05:19.746 --> 00:05:21.980
So, what's inside
the black box is portion

00:05:21.981 --> 00:05:24.735
of the address space is trusted,

00:05:24.736 --> 00:05:28.355
and from the outside
that's untrusted.

00:05:28.356 --> 00:05:30.680
But the trusted portion
can reach outside of

00:05:30.681 --> 00:05:33.280
the black box and access
data for efficiency sake.

00:05:33.281 --> 00:05:36.630
Rather than having to copy
data back and forth inside,

00:05:36.631 --> 00:05:39.500
it can access encrypted data

00:05:39.501 --> 00:05:42.415
that is outside the black box.

00:05:42.416 --> 00:05:44.360
This requires a decomposition of

00:05:44.361 --> 00:05:45.995
the application into
these two pieces,

00:05:45.996 --> 00:05:48.970
the trusted portion and
the untrusted portion.

00:05:48.971 --> 00:05:51.570
So, Azure Confidence Computing,

00:05:51.571 --> 00:05:53.230
looking at these technologies,

00:05:53.231 --> 00:05:56.210
we realize that there's
a lot more than

00:05:56.211 --> 00:05:59.570
just making black boxes or
SGX available to customers,

00:05:59.571 --> 00:06:01.805
or Virtual Secure Mode
available to customers.

00:06:01.806 --> 00:06:04.565
There's a lot of other pieces
that we needed to fill in.

00:06:04.566 --> 00:06:07.040
So, if you take a look at all of

00:06:07.041 --> 00:06:09.405
what we consider
confidential computing in Azure,

00:06:09.406 --> 00:06:10.940
it consists of everything from

00:06:10.941 --> 00:06:13.055
making these enclaves available.

00:06:13.056 --> 00:06:17.270
We now have SGX cluster stood
up in Azure that we make

00:06:17.271 --> 00:06:19.970
available through
a limited preview forum

00:06:19.971 --> 00:06:22.350
for customers that sign up
and want access to them.

00:06:22.351 --> 00:06:24.350
We also are working on

00:06:24.351 --> 00:06:27.110
an SDK that allows developers,
enables developers,

00:06:27.111 --> 00:06:28.715
to develop these
applications that

00:06:28.716 --> 00:06:30.410
are decomposed into
the untrusted and

00:06:30.411 --> 00:06:34.070
trusted portions with
a fairly easy way for

00:06:34.071 --> 00:06:37.920
them to access functions
from outside the black box,

00:06:37.921 --> 00:06:39.150
inside the black box,

00:06:39.151 --> 00:06:42.440
and have the underlying
SDK runtime take care

00:06:42.441 --> 00:06:43.940
of the transport and the

00:06:43.941 --> 00:06:46.775
transitions between the
enclave and the outside world.

00:06:46.776 --> 00:06:49.280
We're also working on an
attestation services,

00:06:49.281 --> 00:06:53.810
is of course necessary for
trusted code on the outside

00:06:53.811 --> 00:06:55.370
to verify the integrity of

00:06:55.371 --> 00:06:59.150
the trusted or code in
the inside of the enclave.

00:06:59.151 --> 00:07:01.790
That attestation that comes
from the enclave provider,

00:07:01.791 --> 00:07:03.530
whether it's the hypervisor or

00:07:03.531 --> 00:07:06.605
the hardware needs to be
validated some place.

00:07:06.606 --> 00:07:08.900
That validation is coming

00:07:08.901 --> 00:07:10.620
in the form of an
attestation service.

00:07:10.621 --> 00:07:11.900
So, we're in the process

00:07:11.901 --> 00:07:13.340
of building an
attestation service,

00:07:13.341 --> 00:07:16.880
one that will attest to
SGX enclaves as well

00:07:16.881 --> 00:07:18.710
as other enclaves
technologies as they come

00:07:18.711 --> 00:07:20.655
out so that the software,

00:07:20.656 --> 00:07:23.210
either inside the data center
or outside the data center,

00:07:23.211 --> 00:07:24.980
can verify the
authenticity integrity

00:07:24.981 --> 00:07:26.860
of this code that's inside
one of these enclaves,

00:07:26.861 --> 00:07:30.400
and that it's actually
a trusted enclave.

00:07:30.401 --> 00:07:33.990
Finally, one
other aspect of it is,

00:07:33.991 --> 00:07:37.440
actually two other aspects
that we need to mention,

00:07:37.441 --> 00:07:40.915
are that we're looking
at also providing

00:07:40.916 --> 00:07:43.420
confidential computing
capabilities inside of

00:07:43.421 --> 00:07:45.980
our PaaS and SaaS
applications as well.

00:07:45.981 --> 00:07:48.110
So, making it very
easy for customers to

00:07:48.111 --> 00:07:50.200
consume these rather than
having to build applications.

00:07:50.201 --> 00:07:52.280
Of course, all these
are necessary from

00:07:52.281 --> 00:07:55.280
the building blocks to create
these applications up into

00:07:55.281 --> 00:07:59.030
finished services
that end-users can take

00:07:59.031 --> 00:08:00.590
advantage of when they're

00:08:00.591 --> 00:08:03.450
accessing their data or
processing their data.

00:08:03.451 --> 00:08:07.070
Then finally, one of
the key aspects of

00:08:07.071 --> 00:08:09.920
the collaboration with
Microsoft Research has been

00:08:09.921 --> 00:08:13.535
looking at how we can
harden these black boxes.

00:08:13.536 --> 00:08:18.120
Most of you know here that
black boxes, these enclaves,

00:08:18.121 --> 00:08:19.540
SGX specifically,

00:08:19.541 --> 00:08:23.455
have been subject to
side channel attacks.

00:08:23.456 --> 00:08:25.700
So, we're looking
at ways to prevent

00:08:25.701 --> 00:08:28.870
leakage of data
outside of an enclave,

00:08:28.871 --> 00:08:32.915
either through side channel
or through corruption

00:08:32.916 --> 00:08:34.565
of the enclaves code or

00:08:34.566 --> 00:08:37.385
exploitation of bugs
in the enclave code.

00:08:37.386 --> 00:08:39.270
That's an ongoing
research problem with

00:08:39.271 --> 00:08:41.570
Microsoft or with MSR.

00:08:41.571 --> 00:08:44.360
So, speaking of the
information leaks,

00:08:44.361 --> 00:08:45.600
the side channel attacks.

00:08:45.601 --> 00:08:47.960
Being able to look
at the execution

00:08:47.961 --> 00:08:49.760
of the enclave from the outside.

00:08:49.761 --> 00:08:53.245
Seeing its behavior through
page faults, memory accesses.

00:08:53.246 --> 00:08:55.040
This is one of the examples

00:08:55.041 --> 00:08:56.930
of a side-channel
leakage that could

00:08:56.931 --> 00:08:58.970
potentially reveal
information about

00:08:58.971 --> 00:09:01.170
the data that's being processed
inside the black box.

00:09:01.171 --> 00:09:03.620
One of the papers that struck me

00:09:03.621 --> 00:09:06.940
as fascinating was one
by Microsoft Research,

00:09:06.941 --> 00:09:08.570
where that by looking
at page faults they

00:09:08.571 --> 00:09:10.520
were able to reverse

00:09:10.521 --> 00:09:12.650
engineer the JPEG images

00:09:12.651 --> 00:09:14.865
being processed by
a JPEG decoder,

00:09:14.866 --> 00:09:16.910
to close enough
where you could get

00:09:16.911 --> 00:09:19.945
a pretty good idea of
what the image look like.

00:09:19.946 --> 00:09:23.030
So, these kinds of
problems we want

00:09:23.031 --> 00:09:26.815
to protect against by
having a verified runtime,

00:09:26.816 --> 00:09:32.220
an instrumented application
that does two things.

00:09:32.221 --> 00:09:33.980
One, prevent the enclave

00:09:33.981 --> 00:09:36.250
from releasing secrets
to the outside.

00:09:36.251 --> 00:09:38.705
The other one is to prevent

00:09:38.706 --> 00:09:41.150
these information leaks
by looking at

00:09:41.151 --> 00:09:43.920
the side channels of
the execution from the outside.

00:09:43.921 --> 00:09:47.550
So, this is just a
simple example here of,

00:09:47.551 --> 00:09:50.110
actually that should be
animating for some reason.

00:09:50.111 --> 00:09:52.505
It's not but the idea is

00:09:52.506 --> 00:09:55.250
the data comes in and
depending on the tree,

00:09:55.251 --> 00:09:56.930
different parts of
the address space or

00:09:56.931 --> 00:09:59.625
different cache lines
or pages are accessed,

00:09:59.626 --> 00:10:02.240
and you can reverse engineer
which paths through

00:10:02.241 --> 00:10:05.430
the decision tree the code took.

00:10:05.431 --> 00:10:08.930
So, I want to show you
an example of one of

00:10:08.931 --> 00:10:12.280
the projects that
MSR Cambridge has ongoing,

00:10:12.281 --> 00:10:14.190
which is oblivious computation.

00:10:14.191 --> 00:10:15.830
The protection against

00:10:15.831 --> 00:10:17.720
these inadvertent
side channel leaks.

00:10:17.721 --> 00:10:20.450
I'm going to show
you by taking a look

00:10:20.451 --> 00:10:24.490
at support vector
machine execution.

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:46.440
Did you change
the password? I did that.

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:51.415
What? No, I did that.

00:10:51.416 --> 00:10:56.280
Sorry about, this it's secure
login here. Too secure.

00:10:56.281 --> 00:10:58.345
&gt;&gt; Of course I know why.

00:10:58.346 --> 00:10:58.935
&gt;&gt; Here it goes.

00:10:58.936 --> 00:11:01.510
&gt;&gt; Because it's not on the power.

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:06.730
&gt;&gt; Okay, so this is the example.

00:11:06.731 --> 00:11:09.950
First trace I'm going to
show you is a tool that

00:11:09.951 --> 00:11:13.800
monitors the execution
of memory accesses,

00:11:13.801 --> 00:11:18.650
encode accesses, of the support
vector machine computation

00:11:18.651 --> 00:11:20.590
as it's processing data.

00:11:20.591 --> 00:11:23.585
You can see for much of the time

00:11:23.586 --> 00:11:28.320
that these traces actually

00:11:28.321 --> 00:11:29.530
do not line up based off

00:11:29.531 --> 00:11:31.150
the two different inputs A and B.

00:11:31.151 --> 00:11:32.610
That is an example of

00:11:32.611 --> 00:11:34.390
how we could look
at the differences,

00:11:34.391 --> 00:11:37.280
or look at the trace
execution history and reverse

00:11:37.281 --> 00:11:41.410
engineer the data being
processed by the SVM algorithm.

00:11:41.630 --> 00:11:45.820
On the other hand, version of

00:11:45.821 --> 00:11:50.630
the SVM algorithm
that's been fed through

00:11:50.631 --> 00:11:53.105
the oblivious
computation compiler

00:11:53.106 --> 00:11:54.700
executes and no matter where we

00:11:54.701 --> 00:11:55.990
stop the trace you can see

00:11:55.991 --> 00:11:58.865
that the trace pattern
is exactly the same,

00:11:58.866 --> 00:12:01.635
for both data inputs A and B.

00:12:01.636 --> 00:12:07.130
So, we view this as one of
the ways that we can help

00:12:07.131 --> 00:12:13.290
our developers protect against

00:12:13.291 --> 00:12:14.685
the leakage of
side channel attacks.

00:12:14.686 --> 00:12:16.050
There's still a tremendous amount

00:12:16.051 --> 00:12:17.520
of research going. In this space.

00:12:17.521 --> 00:12:20.875
There are some performance
overhead, as you would expect,

00:12:20.876 --> 00:12:24.010
to this kind of
oblivious modification of

00:12:24.011 --> 00:12:26.170
the code and that overhead

00:12:26.171 --> 00:12:27.845
varies depending
on the algorithm,

00:12:27.846 --> 00:12:31.535
research work is ongoing
to minimize that overhead.

00:12:31.536 --> 00:12:33.660
Let's talk about
some of the examples

00:12:33.661 --> 00:12:35.450
of competence we're
computing that we're also

00:12:35.451 --> 00:12:37.580
exploring both in
the Azure product group

00:12:37.581 --> 00:12:38.790
as well as with MSR.

00:12:38.791 --> 00:12:40.480
We start with one of them,

00:12:40.481 --> 00:12:43.815
which is always encrypted
storage with SQL server.

00:12:43.816 --> 00:12:45.690
Let me talk a little bit
about that one.

00:12:45.691 --> 00:12:48.780
I'll talk about
confidential blockchains.

00:12:48.781 --> 00:12:50.790
There's financial data
processing and there

00:12:50.791 --> 00:12:53.350
is secure multi-party
machine learning.

00:12:53.351 --> 00:12:56.720
When it comes to SQL server,

00:12:56.721 --> 00:12:58.700
the way the customers
today protect

00:12:58.701 --> 00:13:00.220
their data from access by

00:13:00.221 --> 00:13:01.800
the Cloud provider
or by the host,

00:13:01.801 --> 00:13:04.940
or it is feature called
SQL is always encrypted,

00:13:04.941 --> 00:13:08.280
where they have the key
on the client side,

00:13:08.281 --> 00:13:12.255
the SQL runtime, Client Runtime

00:13:12.256 --> 00:13:14.740
transparently takes
that key and encrypts

00:13:14.741 --> 00:13:16.290
data on the way
into the SQL server

00:13:16.291 --> 00:13:17.665
and decrypts it on the way out.

00:13:17.666 --> 00:13:19.130
So, the data is always in

00:13:19.131 --> 00:13:22.445
encrypted form on
the back-end, in the server.

00:13:22.446 --> 00:13:24.930
The downsides of this though are

00:13:24.931 --> 00:13:29.650
that the SQL server is
not able to see the data,

00:13:29.651 --> 00:13:31.410
and therefore not able to perform

00:13:31.411 --> 00:13:33.350
rich expressions on
top of the data.

00:13:33.351 --> 00:13:35.980
Some simple comparisons,
if you're using

00:13:35.981 --> 00:13:38.600
a symmetric encryption
algorithm like

00:13:38.601 --> 00:13:42.550
equality are possible but
certainly not ranges.

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:48.250
So, what Confidential SQL
version of Always Encrypted,

00:13:48.251 --> 00:13:49.440
which is something
we've announced it's

00:13:49.441 --> 00:13:50.750
coming and we're working with

00:13:50.751 --> 00:13:54.625
the SQL product team to
get into released form.

00:13:54.626 --> 00:13:58.320
The SQL data is processed inside

00:13:58.321 --> 00:14:03.430
the database's engine is
executing inside of an enclave.

00:14:03.431 --> 00:14:09.070
The data is encrypted on
the way into the Cloud and then

00:14:09.071 --> 00:14:11.420
the keys are released
after attestation of

00:14:11.421 --> 00:14:12.870
the SQL engine to

00:14:12.871 --> 00:14:14.880
the SQL engine so that
it can process the data.

00:14:14.881 --> 00:14:18.060
In this case, you get
arbitrary rich expressions

00:14:18.061 --> 00:14:22.390
just like you would with
unencrypted version of SQL.

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:25.670
The data remains
encrypted in flight,

00:14:25.671 --> 00:14:28.105
it remains encrypted on

00:14:28.106 --> 00:14:31.385
disk and it remains encrypted
as it's been computed over.

00:14:31.386 --> 00:14:34.120
Another scenario like I've

00:14:34.121 --> 00:14:36.730
mentioned is
Confidential Blockchain.

00:14:36.731 --> 00:14:40.350
If you take a look at the way
blockchains are evolved,

00:14:40.351 --> 00:14:45.090
they started with absolutely no
confidentiality at all.

00:14:45.091 --> 00:14:47.405
So, the Bitcoin and
Ethereum networks,

00:14:47.406 --> 00:14:49.070
everybody that's participating in

00:14:49.071 --> 00:14:50.900
that network can see
everything that's going on.

00:14:50.901 --> 00:14:52.580
In fact, that's a property

00:14:52.581 --> 00:14:55.500
that's necessary for
the security of those networks.

00:14:55.501 --> 00:14:57.635
Over time we've seen

00:14:57.636 --> 00:14:59.930
different paths being

00:14:59.931 --> 00:15:02.380
explored for providing
confidentiality.

00:15:02.381 --> 00:15:05.660
Like side chains and like
zero-knowledge proofs.

00:15:05.661 --> 00:15:08.630
All of these add
a significant amount of

00:15:08.631 --> 00:15:12.160
complexity to the interactions
with the blockchain

00:15:12.161 --> 00:15:13.900
and the construction of the
blockchain and they have

00:15:13.901 --> 00:15:16.580
limitations as well in terms of

00:15:16.581 --> 00:15:19.480
their scalability and how much

00:15:19.481 --> 00:15:22.640
data then can be shared
by the network itself

00:15:22.641 --> 00:15:24.920
versus sent off into a side chain

00:15:24.921 --> 00:15:27.390
that only the parties involved
with that can see and

00:15:27.391 --> 00:15:29.890
therefore makes it very
difficult to take that data off

00:15:29.891 --> 00:15:31.180
those side chains and make it

00:15:31.181 --> 00:15:33.895
available to other parties
that weren't part of that.

00:15:33.896 --> 00:15:37.540
Further, zero-knowledge
proofs, for example,

00:15:37.541 --> 00:15:39.885
have a high
computational overhead,

00:15:39.886 --> 00:15:41.090
and so that will slow down the

00:15:41.091 --> 00:15:43.085
execution of the blockchain.

00:15:43.086 --> 00:15:47.060
By placing the blockchain
network within a network of

00:15:47.061 --> 00:15:49.270
Trusted Execution environments
where they test to

00:15:49.271 --> 00:15:51.980
each other the code
that they're running,

00:15:51.981 --> 00:15:54.380
the parties that are governing

00:15:54.381 --> 00:15:58.675
the individual nodes can
certify which code they trust,

00:15:58.676 --> 00:16:00.920
including the blockchain code as

00:16:00.921 --> 00:16:03.420
well as the CoCo runtime code,

00:16:03.421 --> 00:16:06.090
the CoCo being
the project name for this,

00:16:06.091 --> 00:16:08.585
which stands for
confidential consortium,

00:16:08.586 --> 00:16:11.720
that code establishes this
network and then implements

00:16:11.721 --> 00:16:14.140
the basic run-time
primitives that

00:16:14.141 --> 00:16:17.100
are blockchain ledger can
be layered on top of.

00:16:17.101 --> 00:16:19.310
This includes secure storage of

00:16:19.311 --> 00:16:21.820
the blockchain network on disk as

00:16:21.821 --> 00:16:24.220
well as the secure release

00:16:24.221 --> 00:16:25.020
of this information to

00:16:25.021 --> 00:16:26.640
parties that should
have access to,

00:16:26.641 --> 00:16:28.310
which makes possible
a few things.

00:16:28.311 --> 00:16:31.855
One of them, extremely high
transaction throughput and

00:16:31.856 --> 00:16:33.790
extremely low latency because

00:16:33.791 --> 00:16:36.135
once you end up trusting
the nodes in this way,

00:16:36.136 --> 00:16:39.350
the nodes trust each other
you can fall back to crash

00:16:39.351 --> 00:16:42.035
tolerant consensus
algorithms as opposed to

00:16:42.036 --> 00:16:46.345
prac pBFT algorithms or
proof-of-work algorithms.

00:16:46.346 --> 00:16:49.620
Further, because everything that

00:16:49.621 --> 00:16:52.405
happens in the Enclave
is trusted,

00:16:52.406 --> 00:16:55.680
you can program arbitrary
confidentiality rules

00:16:55.681 --> 00:16:58.780
into that code as through
governance policy.

00:16:58.781 --> 00:17:01.915
One of the very common scenario
as we've heard is that

00:17:01.916 --> 00:17:03.820
multiple parties are
transacting with

00:17:03.821 --> 00:17:05.995
one another inside
the blockchain,

00:17:05.996 --> 00:17:09.205
they should only be able to
see their own transactions.

00:17:09.206 --> 00:17:11.600
But there's an auditor
that is part of

00:17:11.601 --> 00:17:13.080
this consortium that
needs to be able to

00:17:13.081 --> 00:17:14.820
see the transactions
of multiple parties.

00:17:14.821 --> 00:17:17.105
So, how do you set that
up in a dynamic way?

00:17:17.106 --> 00:17:18.540
It's actually very
straightforward

00:17:18.541 --> 00:17:20.065
if you've got a
network like this,

00:17:20.066 --> 00:17:21.820
because you just implement

00:17:21.821 --> 00:17:23.540
those confidentiality rules
as you would in

00:17:23.541 --> 00:17:25.400
any trusted network
which is simply

00:17:25.401 --> 00:17:27.925
through access control policies,

00:17:27.926 --> 00:17:30.085
where the auditor has access to

00:17:30.086 --> 00:17:31.730
arbitrary transactions
in the code

00:17:31.731 --> 00:17:33.350
inside the enclave that's

00:17:33.351 --> 00:17:37.480
part of CoCo with a blockchain
primitives laid on top of

00:17:37.481 --> 00:17:39.195
it would only make that

00:17:39.196 --> 00:17:42.240
those transactions available
to authorized parties.

00:17:42.241 --> 00:17:43.760
So, confidentiality.

00:17:43.761 --> 00:17:45.505
The governance can
also be built in

00:17:45.506 --> 00:17:49.090
to vote on which parties can be

00:17:49.091 --> 00:17:51.505
added to the blockchain as
well as to evict parties

00:17:51.506 --> 00:17:53.260
all with very high throughput

00:17:53.261 --> 00:17:54.715
and low latency like I mentioned.

00:17:54.716 --> 00:17:58.500
This is a picture of
the CoCo architecture.

00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:01.590
What we've done with CoCo

00:18:01.591 --> 00:18:04.090
is created a prototype
last year that we

00:18:04.091 --> 00:18:05.620
actually demonstrated
publicly when we

00:18:05.621 --> 00:18:08.730
announced CoCo to the world.

00:18:08.731 --> 00:18:11.710
The CoCo prototype
has evolved now

00:18:11.711 --> 00:18:14.580
and we're building
a production version of CoCo.

00:18:14.581 --> 00:18:16.540
This runtime, like I mentioned,

00:18:16.541 --> 00:18:18.715
arbitrary blockchains can
be layered on top of.

00:18:18.716 --> 00:18:20.650
So, this initial prototype we're

00:18:20.651 --> 00:18:23.700
layering of the Ethereum
Blockchain on top of it.

00:18:23.701 --> 00:18:26.565
So, it's compatible with
off the shelf Ethereum,

00:18:26.566 --> 00:18:30.820
though with confidentiality
atributtes added to Ethereum.

00:18:30.821 --> 00:18:31.830
So, you can specify

00:18:31.831 --> 00:18:34.740
confidentiality on top
of smart contracts.

00:18:34.741 --> 00:18:39.285
This project, we intend to
make completely open-source.

00:18:39.286 --> 00:18:42.160
That means that
customers can take this

00:18:42.161 --> 00:18:45.705
and run it on their own servers
outside of Azure.

00:18:45.706 --> 00:18:48.850
The goal here isn't
necessarily to make

00:18:48.851 --> 00:18:50.200
sure that this is locked into

00:18:50.201 --> 00:18:51.970
Azure or Microsoft
only technologies.

00:18:51.971 --> 00:18:53.055
It's open-source.

00:18:53.056 --> 00:18:58.330
The enclave runtime that

00:18:58.331 --> 00:19:01.405
we're working on will support
both Linux and Windows.

00:19:01.406 --> 00:19:03.330
CoCo will eventually support

00:19:03.331 --> 00:19:05.770
both Linux and Windows
and it will be

00:19:05.771 --> 00:19:07.960
published in GitHub
for other parties

00:19:07.961 --> 00:19:10.370
to participate in the
development of it.

00:19:10.371 --> 00:19:13.020
We also expect other
blockchain ledgers

00:19:13.021 --> 00:19:15.815
to be integrated on top of
CoCo over time as well.

00:19:15.816 --> 00:19:19.890
So, let me show you
a quick example of CoCo,

00:19:19.891 --> 00:19:23.660
and what I'm going to do.

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:39.655
I lost track of this
one. Here we go.

00:19:39.656 --> 00:19:40.750
&gt;&gt; Is it locked up again?

00:19:40.751 --> 00:19:45.400
&gt;&gt; No, I don't think so.
Yeah, it's locked up.

00:19:45.401 --> 00:19:48.775
I don't why it's doing this.

00:19:48.776 --> 00:19:54.040
This is SSH session
to a Linux machine,

00:19:54.041 --> 00:19:58.070
that is running in
Azure and SGX machine.

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:11.290
So, what I'm going to
do first is show a

00:20:11.291 --> 00:20:16.335
simple smart contract- thanks.

00:20:16.336 --> 00:20:21.025
So, I'm going to show a simple
smart contract executed on

00:20:21.026 --> 00:20:26.420
the Ethereum CoCo
integrated blockchain.

00:20:26.421 --> 00:20:31.155
This is standing up
a five-node Ethereum network,

00:20:31.156 --> 00:20:35.440
and then we'll execute
as many transactions as it

00:20:35.441 --> 00:20:40.970
can and then show us
the throughput that it achieves.

00:20:42.210 --> 00:20:46.290
Of course on the public
version of Ethereum,

00:20:46.291 --> 00:20:48.935
which is based off of
proof -of-work consensus

00:20:48.936 --> 00:20:50.210
that transactions throughputs

00:20:50.211 --> 00:20:52.310
10-15 transactions per second.

00:20:52.311 --> 00:20:54.250
You can see here that, again,

00:20:54.251 --> 00:20:56.470
this is Ethereum, stock Ethereum,

00:20:56.471 --> 00:20:59.235
integrated with CoCo
and it's achieving,

00:20:59.236 --> 00:21:01.185
you can see around

00:21:01.186 --> 00:21:04.615
12,000 transactions to 13,000
transactions a second.

00:21:04.616 --> 00:21:06.560
We've also, just to push

00:21:06.561 --> 00:21:10.425
the limits of the CoCo Framework,

00:21:10.426 --> 00:21:13.700
just created
a simple ledger network

00:21:13.701 --> 00:21:16.230
and then integrated
that with CoCo,

00:21:16.231 --> 00:21:19.440
and this is standing up
a five-node version of

00:21:19.441 --> 00:21:22.130
that network and then

00:21:22.131 --> 00:21:25.400
also trying to see how
fast we can drive it.

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:28.980
You'll see that it's

00:21:28.981 --> 00:21:30.530
significantly higher
than what we got with

00:21:30.531 --> 00:21:33.270
Ethereum and yet there's

00:21:33.271 --> 00:21:35.420
still more performance
optimization to do.

00:21:35.421 --> 00:21:37.760
So, you can see that we're

00:21:37.761 --> 00:21:40.270
achieving close to 100,000
transactions a second.

00:21:40.271 --> 00:21:42.820
We think we can get up to
150,000 transactions a

00:21:42.821 --> 00:21:47.080
second or higher in
five-node network here.

00:21:50.690 --> 00:21:52.890
Then finally, one of

00:21:52.891 --> 00:21:55.080
the last cases that
we're looking at,

00:21:55.081 --> 00:21:56.150
and this one's very exciting from

00:21:56.151 --> 00:21:57.450
a number of different domains,

00:21:57.451 --> 00:22:01.110
is multi-party confidential
machine learning.

00:22:01.111 --> 00:22:04.160
One of the canonical
scenarios for

00:22:04.161 --> 00:22:07.080
this is the processing
of medical data,

00:22:07.081 --> 00:22:08.540
where different parties
are coming together

00:22:08.541 --> 00:22:11.515
with health information gathered
from different sources,

00:22:11.516 --> 00:22:17.600
whether X-rays, CAT scans,
other diagnostic data.

00:22:17.601 --> 00:22:19.760
These parties are bringing
it together and they can get

00:22:19.761 --> 00:22:22.010
a deeper insight than any one
of them individually can,

00:22:22.011 --> 00:22:24.525
but there's regulatory
restrictions or

00:22:24.526 --> 00:22:26.330
privacy concerns over sharing

00:22:26.331 --> 00:22:27.915
their data with
these other parties.

00:22:27.916 --> 00:22:32.295
With confidential computing
and these enclaves, they can,

00:22:32.296 --> 00:22:34.625
if they trust the algorithm
running inside the enclave,

00:22:34.626 --> 00:22:37.680
release keys that
enable the enclave

00:22:37.681 --> 00:22:39.075
to access their data
and then perform

00:22:39.076 --> 00:22:41.235
this multiparty
computation over it.

00:22:41.236 --> 00:22:42.980
So, I'm going to switch
back and show you

00:22:42.981 --> 00:22:45.060
a very quick demo of that.

00:22:45.061 --> 00:22:49.600
This is the hospital scenario
with three parties.

00:22:49.601 --> 00:22:56.205
So, we have the server.

00:22:56.206 --> 00:22:58.495
This is where
the training will happen.

00:22:58.496 --> 00:23:04.085
We've got Hospital A,
we've got Hospital B,

00:23:04.086 --> 00:23:06.180
and I'll show you Hospital A

00:23:06.181 --> 00:23:08.160
is taking its plain text data,

00:23:08.161 --> 00:23:10.305
it's going to encrypt it,

00:23:10.306 --> 00:23:12.620
and then it's going
to encrypt it with

00:23:12.621 --> 00:23:17.630
the server key that
came from the enclave.

00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:25.765
Now, the server will
process the data,

00:23:25.766 --> 00:23:27.950
show the encrypted model
outside the enclave and

00:23:27.951 --> 00:23:31.030
then when we go
back to Hospital A,

00:23:32.060 --> 00:23:35.405
you can see Hospital B
is getting back

00:23:35.406 --> 00:23:37.260
the encrypted weights and

00:23:37.261 --> 00:23:39.510
decrypting them with it's
own key and then showing them.

00:23:39.511 --> 00:23:40.985
So, in this case,

00:23:40.986 --> 00:23:42.520
Hospital A and Hospital B,

00:23:42.521 --> 00:23:44.540
we're able to get insights out
of the data without either

00:23:44.541 --> 00:23:47.095
one having access to
each other's data,

00:23:47.096 --> 00:23:49.565
just trusting that
machine learning algorithm

00:23:49.566 --> 00:23:50.880
and the Trusted Execution

00:23:50.881 --> 00:23:53.000
Environment it was executing in.

00:23:55.790 --> 00:23:59.980
So, that's a quick look at
confidential computing.

00:23:59.981 --> 00:24:01.750
I fundamentally believe,

00:24:01.751 --> 00:24:03.245
we fundamentally
believe that in Azure,

00:24:03.246 --> 00:24:05.945
that we're at the beginning

00:24:05.946 --> 00:24:07.250
of a transformation of the way

00:24:07.251 --> 00:24:09.410
computing is done
today because of

00:24:09.411 --> 00:24:12.165
limitations in
enclave technology.

00:24:12.166 --> 00:24:14.155
We'll see this only for

00:24:14.156 --> 00:24:16.210
very kind of extreme cases

00:24:16.211 --> 00:24:18.435
of customer
sensitivity over data.

00:24:18.436 --> 00:24:22.000
We expect that this
will unlock data that

00:24:22.001 --> 00:24:23.740
would otherwise not
come to the Cloud

00:24:23.741 --> 00:24:26.100
and allow it to
come to the Cloud,

00:24:26.101 --> 00:24:28.410
but that over time
these primitives

00:24:28.411 --> 00:24:31.230
are powerful enough
that we will see

00:24:31.231 --> 00:24:34.300
the creation of
effectively the equivalent of

00:24:34.301 --> 00:24:37.810
these private data centers
on prem data centers

00:24:37.811 --> 00:24:39.260
within the public cloud

00:24:39.261 --> 00:24:41.995
protected with
enclave technology.

00:24:41.996 --> 00:24:43.610
Also, as part of
our intelligent edge,

00:24:43.611 --> 00:24:45.665
Intelligent Cloud strategy,

00:24:45.666 --> 00:24:47.540
of course enclave
technology will be

00:24:47.541 --> 00:24:49.600
critical on the edge
to protect data,

00:24:49.601 --> 00:24:51.635
sensitive data, as
it's being processed

00:24:51.636 --> 00:24:54.975
on a collected from
those edge devices.

00:24:54.976 --> 00:24:57.550
So, with that, I want to thank

00:24:57.551 --> 00:24:59.630
you very much for taking a look

