&gt;&gt; Let's thank Mark. I think

00:25:42.951 --> 00:25:45.015
we have time for a
couple of questions,

00:25:45.016 --> 00:25:47.205
and then all the speakers
will be around.

00:25:47.206 --> 00:25:50.110
So, but let's keep
the questions short.

00:25:50.111 --> 00:25:54.460
Let's take two questions. Yeah.

00:25:54.461 --> 00:25:57.625
&gt;&gt; Can you please comment

00:25:57.626 --> 00:25:59.810
on how you will extend

00:25:59.811 --> 00:26:02.760
these enclave technologies
to accelerators,

00:26:02.761 --> 00:26:04.225
given that Azure is now

00:26:04.226 --> 00:26:06.860
incorporating accelerators
and there're more to come.

00:26:06.861 --> 00:26:09.755
&gt;&gt; This is an area
of ongoing research.

00:26:09.756 --> 00:26:12.140
I don't really have anything
more to say about it

00:26:12.141 --> 00:26:14.305
other than we are
exploring how to do that.

00:26:14.306 --> 00:26:16.890
Of course, the machine
learning example

00:26:16.891 --> 00:26:18.725
highlighted the need for that,

00:26:18.726 --> 00:26:21.075
that the training which is,

00:26:21.076 --> 00:26:23.760
not best done on CPUs,

00:26:23.761 --> 00:26:25.990
but done accelerators, is one of

00:26:25.991 --> 00:26:29.520
the scenarios that we're
looking at very closely.

00:26:30.020 --> 00:26:32.800
&gt;&gt; One more question.

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:36.235
&gt;&gt; Maybe just an answer to that.

00:26:36.236 --> 00:26:39.655
This morning Stavros
Pineios was presenting.

00:26:39.656 --> 00:26:43.675
So, there is a tech demo
in doing that.

00:26:43.676 --> 00:26:45.265
You can find Stavros

00:26:45.266 --> 00:26:48.310
and he can tell you a little bit
about his research.

00:26:48.311 --> 00:26:50.620
There was a small pic
this morning

00:26:50.621 --> 00:26:51.970
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:26:51.971 --> 00:26:52.675
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:26:52.676 --> 00:26:54.860
&gt;&gt; I have a quick question.
Could you please

00:26:54.861 --> 00:26:57.070
comment something on the use of

00:26:57.071 --> 00:26:59.370
homomorphic computing in Azure

00:26:59.371 --> 00:27:02.345
and how practical that
is now, nowadays.

00:27:02.346 --> 00:27:03.820
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, that's
another big effort in

00:27:03.821 --> 00:27:05.740
Microsoft researchers,
homomorphic encryption,

00:27:05.741 --> 00:27:07.220
and we watched the development

00:27:07.221 --> 00:27:08.680
of that very closely as well,

00:27:08.681 --> 00:27:10.410
it's got some limitations and has

00:27:10.411 --> 00:27:13.920
performance overheads as
well associated with it.

00:27:13.921 --> 00:27:15.705
We don't think necessarily
this is one size,

00:27:15.706 --> 00:27:17.645
fits all space.

00:27:17.646 --> 00:27:21.135
In fact, homomorphic encryption
inside an enclave,

00:27:21.136 --> 00:27:22.540
the combination of the two

00:27:22.541 --> 00:27:24.305
is something we're
also looking at,

00:27:24.306 --> 00:27:28.910
as ways to extend the power
of homomorphic encryption.

00:27:28.911 --> 00:27:31.470
&gt;&gt; Right. Let's thank Mark
again, thank you very much.

00:27:31.471 --> 00:27:32.700
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:27:34.150 --> 00:27:38.025
&gt;&gt; So, our next speaker
is Dawn Song.

00:27:38.026 --> 00:27:39.690
Dawn is a Professor in

00:27:39.691 --> 00:27:41.470
the Electrical Engineering and

00:27:41.471 --> 00:27:43.355
Computer Science at Berkeley.

00:27:43.356 --> 00:27:47.255
Dawn has a very broad range
of interesting security,

00:27:47.256 --> 00:27:49.080
ranging from software security,

00:27:49.081 --> 00:27:51.355
network security,
distributed systems,

00:27:51.356 --> 00:27:52.714
applied crypto,

00:27:52.715 --> 00:27:57.060
blockchain, and
machine learning and security.

00:27:57.061 --> 00:28:01.100
Dawn received a very
large number of awards,

00:28:01.101 --> 00:28:03.605
including the
MacArthur Fellowship,

00:28:03.606 --> 00:28:07.390
Guggenheim Fellowship,
the NSF CAREER Award,

00:28:07.391 --> 00:28:09.345
Sloan Research Fellowship,

00:28:09.346 --> 00:28:13.860
MIT Technology Review
award, and many others.

00:28:13.861 --> 00:28:17.480
I would highlight that Dawn is

00:28:17.481 --> 00:28:21.510
ranked as the most cited scholar
in computer security,

00:28:21.511 --> 00:28:23.910
so that's a special achievement.

00:28:23.911 --> 00:28:26.590
So, we are very lucky to
have Dawn here today,

00:28:26.591 --> 00:28:28.855
thank you very much, and
I'll hand over to you.

00:28:28.856 --> 00:28:30.335
&gt;&gt; Okay. Yeah, thank you.

00:28:30.336 --> 00:28:32.325
Thank you everyone
for being here.

00:28:32.326 --> 00:28:35.620
Today, I'll talk about
towards secure, practical,

00:28:35.621 --> 00:28:40.030
confidential computing with
open-source secure enclave.

00:28:40.031 --> 00:28:42.790
Now, wearing two hats,

00:28:42.791 --> 00:28:44.950
I'm a professor at UC Berkeley in

00:28:44.951 --> 00:28:47.140
Computer Science
Department and also,

00:28:47.141 --> 00:28:48.670
I'm the founder and CEO of

00:28:48.671 --> 00:28:51.830
a new startup called Oasis Labs.

00:28:52.410 --> 00:28:55.390
So, here we'll hear a lot about

00:28:55.391 --> 00:28:57.730
the value of data analytics
and machine learning,

00:28:57.731 --> 00:29:00.505
they can really bring us

00:29:00.506 --> 00:29:03.910
a greater power in terms
of gaining insights

00:29:03.911 --> 00:29:05.920
from data and to help us to make

00:29:05.921 --> 00:29:07.360
better decisions in

00:29:07.361 --> 00:29:10.600
many different areas
including healthcare,

00:29:10.601 --> 00:29:14.140
IoT, smart building,

00:29:14.141 --> 00:29:17.650
and financial services,
many different areas.

00:29:17.651 --> 00:29:19.525
Also, as we all know,

00:29:19.526 --> 00:29:21.670
data is the new oil.

00:29:21.671 --> 00:29:25.120
Unfortunately, a lot of this
data also is very sensitive.

00:29:25.121 --> 00:29:27.340
So, as we use the data
we need to be very,

00:29:27.341 --> 00:29:31.330
very careful with
protecting user's privacy.

00:29:31.331 --> 00:29:34.435
So, first let's
look at how current

00:29:34.436 --> 00:29:37.795
framework works for data
analytics and machine learning.

00:29:37.796 --> 00:29:40.900
So, how current
framework works is

00:29:40.901 --> 00:29:43.990
that users data is
being collected,

00:29:43.991 --> 00:29:45.610
and then the analysts will write

00:29:45.611 --> 00:29:47.845
analytics and
machine learning program.

00:29:47.846 --> 00:29:49.540
This program will then run

00:29:49.541 --> 00:29:51.400
on that computation
infrastructure,

00:29:51.401 --> 00:29:53.860
which then computes
their results.

00:29:53.861 --> 00:29:57.415
However, the current framework is

00:29:57.416 --> 00:30:00.970
insufficient for our security
and privacy protection.

00:30:00.971 --> 00:30:03.070
For a number of reasons and in

00:30:03.071 --> 00:30:06.640
particular several
different types of threats.

00:30:06.641 --> 00:30:10.735
The three types of
threats is that here,

00:30:10.736 --> 00:30:12.460
the analytics and
machine learning program

00:30:12.461 --> 00:30:14.050
itself actually maybe

00:30:14.051 --> 00:30:18.100
untrusted because they can
be inside the attacks,

00:30:18.101 --> 00:30:20.620
the analysts account may
have been compromised,

00:30:20.621 --> 00:30:23.110
hence the attacker
can actually inject

00:30:23.111 --> 00:30:25.300
a malicious program into

00:30:25.301 --> 00:30:29.410
the analytics and
machine learning program.

00:30:29.411 --> 00:30:32.950
Also, the second threat is

00:30:32.951 --> 00:30:34.825
the computation
infrastructure itself

00:30:34.826 --> 00:30:36.760
maybe untrusted as well.

00:30:36.761 --> 00:30:38.455
Again, attackers may have

00:30:38.456 --> 00:30:41.740
compromised the computation
infrastructure and hence,

00:30:41.741 --> 00:30:44.140
can then steal sensitive data

00:30:44.141 --> 00:30:46.540
during the computation process.

00:30:46.541 --> 00:30:49.420
Finally, the computation result

00:30:49.421 --> 00:30:51.745
itself can reveal
sensitive information

00:30:51.746 --> 00:30:52.810
about the inputs as

00:30:52.811 --> 00:30:56.560
well because these computation
results actually are

00:30:56.561 --> 00:31:01.180
computed from the sensitive
inputs to start with,

00:31:01.181 --> 00:31:03.730
and hence the computation
result itself

00:31:03.731 --> 00:31:06.865
may reveal sensitive information

00:31:06.866 --> 00:31:08.735
about the inputs as well.

00:31:08.736 --> 00:31:12.525
So, to adjust all these three
different types of threats,

00:31:12.526 --> 00:31:15.345
we need to develop
different types of technologies,

00:31:15.346 --> 00:31:17.400
and this is something that's in

00:31:17.401 --> 00:31:20.030
my group has been working at in.

00:31:20.031 --> 00:31:25.285
So, first to address

00:31:25.286 --> 00:31:29.110
the threats of
computation outputs

00:31:29.111 --> 00:31:32.605
revealing sensitive information
about the inputs,

00:31:32.606 --> 00:31:35.050
we have been developing

00:31:35.051 --> 00:31:39.340
technologies in computing
practical solutions for

00:31:39.341 --> 00:31:43.525
differential privacy
and for adjusting

00:31:43.526 --> 00:31:48.460
the threats of
untrusted data analytics

00:31:48.461 --> 00:31:49.930
and machine learning program,

00:31:49.931 --> 00:31:52.150
and we have been
developing technologies

00:31:52.151 --> 00:31:54.280
for our program rewriting
and verification,

00:31:54.281 --> 00:31:56.200
so that we can actually

00:31:56.201 --> 00:31:58.870
automatically rewrite
the data analytics and

00:31:58.871 --> 00:32:02.395
machine learning
pipelines to ensure that

00:32:02.396 --> 00:32:05.310
even though
the original analytics

00:32:05.311 --> 00:32:07.650
and machine learning program
maybe untrusted,

00:32:07.651 --> 00:32:10.170
the rewritten program will

00:32:10.171 --> 00:32:12.210
ensures that it will satisfy

00:32:12.211 --> 00:32:15.430
the desired security and
privacy requirements.

00:32:15.870 --> 00:32:20.350
To address the threats of
untrusted infrastructure,

00:32:20.351 --> 00:32:24.790
we leverage technologies
for a secure computation.

00:32:24.791 --> 00:32:28.405
Given this session is on
confidential computing,

00:32:28.406 --> 00:32:30.925
this is the area that we will

00:32:30.926 --> 00:32:33.985
actually focus more
in in this talk,

00:32:33.986 --> 00:32:36.055
the secure computation side.

00:32:36.056 --> 00:32:40.450
I wanted to show this overall
picture just to explain

00:32:40.451 --> 00:32:44.470
that when we do data analytics
and machine learning,

00:32:44.471 --> 00:32:50.035
the privacy and security aspect
is actually fairly broad.

00:32:50.036 --> 00:32:53.275
We need to actually protect

00:32:53.276 --> 00:32:54.820
the security and privacy of

00:32:54.821 --> 00:32:56.965
data from many different angles,

00:32:56.966 --> 00:32:58.870
and not just
confidential computing,

00:32:58.871 --> 00:33:00.610
and it's important so that we

00:33:00.611 --> 00:33:03.175
actually combine these
different technologies

00:33:03.176 --> 00:33:05.440
to really ensure

00:33:05.441 --> 00:33:08.290
the security and privacy
protection for user's data.

00:33:08.291 --> 00:33:09.565
But for this talk,

00:33:09.566 --> 00:33:11.095
we will mainly focus

00:33:11.096 --> 00:33:15.145
on adjusting the second type
of threats listed here,

00:33:15.146 --> 00:33:17.515
using secure computation to

00:33:17.516 --> 00:33:21.370
protect against
the untrusted infrastructure.

00:33:21.371 --> 00:33:25.375
So, the concept of secure
computation is very simple,

00:33:25.376 --> 00:33:27.400
the goal is
essentially to be able

00:33:27.401 --> 00:33:29.530
to simulate
a trusted third party,

00:33:29.531 --> 00:33:32.965
where essentially user's data

00:33:32.966 --> 00:33:36.565
is being used to compute
a certain function.

00:33:36.566 --> 00:33:40.360
With this simulated
trusted third party,

00:33:40.361 --> 00:33:43.945
we can ensure that from the
computation perspective,

00:33:43.946 --> 00:33:47.545
user's data is being protected.

00:33:47.546 --> 00:33:53.665
So, an attacker will not
be able to see more than,

00:33:53.666 --> 00:33:56.780
in this case, the
computation results.

00:33:58.050 --> 00:34:00.220
Also in this user,

00:34:00.221 --> 00:34:02.470
we also want to ensure
that the right program is

00:34:02.471 --> 00:34:07.400
being executed and it
does the right things.

00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:11.800
One example illustrating
the importance of

00:34:11.801 --> 00:34:14.770
secure computation
is, for example,

00:34:14.771 --> 00:34:17.500
we want to build

00:34:17.501 --> 00:34:19.585
a word-embedding model from

00:34:19.586 --> 00:34:22.120
user's text messages,
and, of course,

00:34:22.121 --> 00:34:25.180
user's text messages
are really sensitive,

00:34:25.181 --> 00:34:28.600
and the computation infrastructure
may not be trusted,

00:34:28.601 --> 00:34:30.400
so we want to enable

00:34:30.401 --> 00:34:32.230
the secure computation
so that we can

00:34:32.231 --> 00:34:34.075
build the language model,

00:34:34.076 --> 00:34:35.860
in this case, while preserving

00:34:35.861 --> 00:34:39.140
the privacy of
user's text messages.

00:34:39.480 --> 00:34:43.285
When we talk about
secure computation,

00:34:43.286 --> 00:34:48.085
there are two main types
of tech approaches.

00:34:48.086 --> 00:34:52.390
The first one is
cryptography-based approach,

00:34:52.391 --> 00:34:56.720
and the second type is
hardware-based approach.

00:34:57.240 --> 00:35:00.370
&gt;&gt; So, with the crypto-based
secure competition,

00:35:00.371 --> 00:35:02.005
there are different techniques,

00:35:02.006 --> 00:35:04.630
including fully-homomorphic
encryption as

00:35:04.631 --> 00:35:07.300
well as secure
multi-party computation.

00:35:07.301 --> 00:35:12.850
The challenge of a
crypto-based secure computation

00:35:12.851 --> 00:35:16.180
is that the performance
overheads can be very high.

00:35:16.181 --> 00:35:19.300
Oftentimes it's
orders of magnitudes

00:35:19.301 --> 00:35:23.620
performance overheads compared
to native computation.

00:35:23.621 --> 00:35:27.175
Because of this, it's
significance and it limits

00:35:27.176 --> 00:35:33.380
the usage scenario for
crypto-based secure computation.

00:35:33.381 --> 00:35:35.905
The second type of approach

00:35:35.906 --> 00:35:38.155
for a secure computation
is hardware-based,

00:35:38.156 --> 00:35:41.770
in particular leveraging
secure hardware to enable

00:35:41.771 --> 00:35:45.669
abstraction that we call
Trusted Execution Environment,

00:35:45.670 --> 00:35:47.530
also called a Secure Enclave.

00:35:47.531 --> 00:35:51.865
So, in this case a
Secure Enclave can provide

00:35:51.866 --> 00:35:55.795
the capability of
hardware-based isolation

00:35:55.796 --> 00:35:58.135
as well as attestation.

00:35:58.136 --> 00:36:01.435
The advantage of secure
hardware is that

00:36:01.436 --> 00:36:04.449
it can provide
a really great performance,

00:36:04.450 --> 00:36:06.745
and oftentimes the performance

00:36:06.746 --> 00:36:11.120
can be very close to
native computation.

00:36:11.970 --> 00:36:15.640
Hardware-based as a computation
essentially can enable

00:36:15.641 --> 00:36:18.370
a very general-purpose
computation and can

00:36:18.371 --> 00:36:23.110
enable many different
security applications.

00:36:23.700 --> 00:36:29.995
So, with the Trusted
Execution Environment,

00:36:29.996 --> 00:36:33.160
we essentially can
run a program inside

00:36:33.161 --> 00:36:35.650
a Secure Enclave and then

00:36:35.651 --> 00:36:38.245
in this case the operating system

00:36:38.246 --> 00:36:41.410
and other processes
running outside of

00:36:41.411 --> 00:36:44.290
the Secure Enclave they
won't be able to tamper with

00:36:44.291 --> 00:36:47.335
the execution of
the program running inside,

00:36:47.336 --> 00:36:49.600
and also they won't be able

00:36:49.601 --> 00:36:52.045
to learn what's running inside,

00:36:52.046 --> 00:36:54.580
ideally, and hence it

00:36:54.581 --> 00:36:58.970
provides the integrity
and confidentiality.

00:36:59.130 --> 00:37:03.820
Also Secure Enclave
can, in this case,

00:37:03.821 --> 00:37:07.675
provide a hardware-based
remote attestation capability,

00:37:07.676 --> 00:37:12.580
where the Secure Enclave
can attest to a remote user

00:37:12.581 --> 00:37:15.160
what program is running inside

00:37:15.161 --> 00:37:18.680
the Secure Enclave and
what is initial state.

00:37:18.990 --> 00:37:22.600
So, Secure Enclave can serve as

00:37:22.601 --> 00:37:25.075
a really strong
cornerstone security

00:37:25.076 --> 00:37:29.080
primitive for us to build
secure systems on top of.

00:37:29.081 --> 00:37:32.250
It can provide very strong
security capabilities

00:37:32.251 --> 00:37:35.550
including authenticate
itself, the device,

00:37:35.551 --> 00:37:38.010
authenticates what program
is running inside,

00:37:38.011 --> 00:37:40.290
and providing the integrity and

00:37:40.291 --> 00:37:43.800
confidentiality of the execution.

00:37:43.801 --> 00:37:47.020
It can really be
a platform for building

00:37:47.021 --> 00:37:49.540
new security applications
that couldn't be built

00:37:49.541 --> 00:37:52.840
otherwise for the same
practical performance.

00:37:52.841 --> 00:37:57.280
There has been many example
security applications

00:37:57.281 --> 00:38:00.400
and systems built on
top of Secure Enclave,

00:38:00.401 --> 00:38:02.754
for example using Intel SGX,

00:38:02.755 --> 00:38:07.940
including a lot of great
work down here at Microsoft.

00:38:08.220 --> 00:38:14.559
So, besides these example
security applications

00:38:14.560 --> 00:38:17.095
that have been demonstrated

00:38:17.096 --> 00:38:19.420
the Secure Enclave can enable,

00:38:19.421 --> 00:38:21.940
also here I want to
just briefly mention

00:38:21.941 --> 00:38:25.360
another user scenario
where Secure Enclave

00:38:25.361 --> 00:38:29.350
can really help solving
even greater problems.

00:38:29.351 --> 00:38:31.690
So here, many nations

00:38:31.691 --> 00:38:35.035
state leaders have
recognized the importance of

00:38:35.036 --> 00:38:37.240
A.I. and stated that "Whoever

00:38:37.241 --> 00:38:40.645
controls and leads in A.I.
will rule the world."

00:38:40.646 --> 00:38:44.200
So, the question is,
who is controlling and

00:38:44.201 --> 00:38:45.700
leading A.I. today and who will

00:38:45.701 --> 00:38:48.220
be ruling the world tomorrow?

00:38:48.221 --> 00:38:52.315
So here, this is the
status quo today.

00:38:52.316 --> 00:38:59.815
Users data is being collected
and big companies build.

00:38:59.816 --> 00:39:02.830
Personnel profiles for users

00:39:02.831 --> 00:39:06.595
and then use these personal
profiles to serve users,

00:39:06.596 --> 00:39:09.700
for example providing
driving directions and even

00:39:09.701 --> 00:39:13.705
giving a job device
and light planes.

00:39:13.706 --> 00:39:25.980
However, currently in
the setting, these services,

00:39:25.981 --> 00:39:30.735
their goal is to maximize
company revenue.

00:39:30.736 --> 00:39:32.730
So, the question is,

00:39:32.731 --> 00:39:34.860
is there a different future

00:39:34.861 --> 00:39:37.240
where we can build

00:39:37.241 --> 00:39:39.880
intelligent agents and
virtual assistants that are

00:39:39.881 --> 00:39:42.295
actually under user control

00:39:42.296 --> 00:39:45.490
they can leverage
services provided by

00:39:45.491 --> 00:39:50.185
third parties but they

00:39:50.186 --> 00:39:54.580
maintain the user profile and
there will be several uses,

00:39:54.581 --> 00:39:58.120
driving directions, Java
device and light planes,

00:39:58.121 --> 00:40:00.640
and the important difference
here is that they will be

00:40:00.641 --> 00:40:05.470
operating under the modes
of maximizing user value.

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:09.850
So, the question is,
is this possible?

00:40:09.851 --> 00:40:13.160
How can we actually achieve this?

00:40:13.560 --> 00:40:16.255
So, as I mentioned,

00:40:16.256 --> 00:40:17.560
I wear two hats.

00:40:17.561 --> 00:40:20.095
So, at Oasis, we are building

00:40:20.096 --> 00:40:22.480
a new technology called the

00:40:22.481 --> 00:40:26.005
privacy-preserving smart
contracts at scale.

00:40:26.006 --> 00:40:28.780
So, what do we do
here is that we are

00:40:28.781 --> 00:40:31.675
building a new
blockchain platform,

00:40:31.676 --> 00:40:34.030
and on top of the
blockchain platform one of

00:40:34.031 --> 00:40:36.040
the key primitive and
capability that will

00:40:36.041 --> 00:40:37.585
provide is what we

00:40:37.586 --> 00:40:40.210
call privacy preserving
smart contracts.

00:40:40.211 --> 00:40:43.660
This privacy preserving
smart contract has

00:40:43.661 --> 00:40:47.695
a number of unique
capabilities and properties.

00:40:47.696 --> 00:40:50.350
It can provide
automatic enforcements

00:40:50.351 --> 00:40:52.510
of codified privacy requirements.

00:40:52.511 --> 00:40:54.490
So, in this case,
the privacy requirements

00:40:54.491 --> 00:40:56.710
actually it will be encode

00:40:56.711 --> 00:41:00.220
this as codes inside

00:41:00.221 --> 00:41:03.040
their privacy preserving
smart contracts,

00:41:03.041 --> 00:41:04.720
and also we can achieve

00:41:04.721 --> 00:41:08.635
these enforcements without
relying on any central party.

00:41:08.636 --> 00:41:11.020
We plan to build

00:41:11.021 --> 00:41:14.590
this privacy-preserving
smart contracts to be able

00:41:14.591 --> 00:41:16.945
to scale to real-world
applications

00:41:16.946 --> 00:41:20.590
including machine learning
even at class scale.

00:41:20.591 --> 00:41:25.540
This primitive of
privacy-preserving smart contracts

00:41:25.541 --> 00:41:27.850
can make it much easier for

00:41:27.851 --> 00:41:29.590
its developers to build

00:41:29.591 --> 00:41:32.590
privacy-preserving
applications without having

00:41:32.591 --> 00:41:36.050
to be a privacy expert.

00:41:38.810 --> 00:41:41.185
In order to enable

00:41:41.186 --> 00:41:44.050
this privacy-preserving
smart contracts at scale,

00:41:44.051 --> 00:41:47.360
the Oasis blockchain platform

00:41:52.470 --> 00:41:55.405
uses a number of
different technologies

00:41:55.406 --> 00:41:58.465
to provide this capability,

00:41:58.466 --> 00:42:01.990
including in a confidentiality

00:42:01.991 --> 00:42:04.044
preserving smart
contract execution,

00:42:04.045 --> 00:42:08.350
which essentially
protects the execution of

00:42:08.351 --> 00:42:09.880
this smart contract from leaking

00:42:09.881 --> 00:42:14.380
sensitive information during
the computation process.

00:42:14.381 --> 00:42:17.665
To also add application layer,

00:42:17.666 --> 00:42:19.570
it provides
privacy-preserving data

00:42:19.571 --> 00:42:20.890
analytics and machine learning,

00:42:20.891 --> 00:42:26.080
including providing
differential privacy for

00:42:26.081 --> 00:42:28.225
the application to prevent

00:42:28.226 --> 00:42:30.880
the output after computation

00:42:30.881 --> 00:42:34.045
from leaking sensitive
information about the inputs.

00:42:34.046 --> 00:42:39.160
We also developed
new architecture and

00:42:39.161 --> 00:42:40.495
technology for

00:42:40.496 --> 00:42:44.075
the blockchain platform
to enable scalability,

00:42:44.076 --> 00:42:47.650
to enable scalable
smart contract execution.

00:42:47.760 --> 00:42:52.570
With this privacy preserving
smart contracts technology,

00:42:52.571 --> 00:42:55.840
we can build blockchain

00:42:55.841 --> 00:42:57.850
of intelligent and
smart contracts,

00:42:57.851 --> 00:42:59.380
where we can actually have

00:42:59.381 --> 00:43:01.840
various machining smart contracts

00:43:01.841 --> 00:43:03.340
that are actually run on top of

00:43:03.341 --> 00:43:06.025
the blockchain and
Oasis blockchain platform,

00:43:06.026 --> 00:43:09.100
and these machining smart
contracts essentially can

00:43:09.101 --> 00:43:12.340
help pull data from

00:43:12.341 --> 00:43:20.199
potentially mutually distrusted
data sources together,

00:43:20.200 --> 00:43:21.490
then build, for example,

00:43:21.491 --> 00:43:24.265
differentially private
financial service models,

00:43:24.266 --> 00:43:26.410
fraud detection models
and even medical

00:43:26.411 --> 00:43:29.930
diagnosis and treatment models.

00:43:30.810 --> 00:43:33.895
So, one of the key
capabilities of

00:43:33.896 --> 00:43:37.480
enabling this privacy
preserving smart contracts is

00:43:37.481 --> 00:43:39.235
the ability to do

00:43:39.236 --> 00:43:44.510
confidentiality preserving
smart contract execution.

00:43:44.820 --> 00:43:51.030
One of the key ingredients to
enable this confidentiality

00:43:51.031 --> 00:43:53.690
preserving smart
contract execution is to

00:43:53.691 --> 00:43:57.570
leverage the capability
of secure hardware.

00:43:58.110 --> 00:44:00.820
All the hardware
manufacturers have

00:44:00.821 --> 00:44:03.685
recognized the importance of
building secure hardware.

00:44:03.686 --> 00:44:07.000
So, over the years, they
have all been developing

00:44:07.001 --> 00:44:08.710
different solutions for

00:44:08.711 --> 00:44:12.310
a secure hardware
including Intel SGX.

00:44:12.311 --> 00:44:15.580
However, today we still

00:44:15.581 --> 00:44:19.870
have lots of open challenges
in building secure hardware.

00:44:19.871 --> 00:44:24.340
So first, how secure can that
be and what threat model,

00:44:24.341 --> 00:44:27.730
and what would you entrust
with a secure hardware?

00:44:27.731 --> 00:44:30.220
For example, you
have bitcoin keys,

00:44:30.221 --> 00:44:33.100
you have financial data,
and you have health data.

00:44:33.101 --> 00:44:36.520
In fact, this is, I will call,

00:44:36.521 --> 00:44:39.970
a grand challenge is
can we actually build

00:44:39.971 --> 00:44:42.430
a truly trustworthy
Secure Enclave that

00:44:42.431 --> 00:44:44.980
can serve as a cornerstone
security primitive,

00:44:44.981 --> 00:44:46.760
that can be widely deployed

00:44:46.761 --> 00:44:50.300
and enable secure systems
to be built on top.

00:44:50.301 --> 00:44:52.270
If we can achieve this,

00:44:52.271 --> 00:44:54.295
it can truly usher

00:44:54.296 --> 00:44:59.575
the entire community into
a new secure computation error.

00:44:59.576 --> 00:45:04.090
So, what's the path to trust
with a Secure Enclave?

00:45:04.091 --> 00:45:07.075
There are several
important steps.

00:45:07.076 --> 00:45:09.970
So first, we need to
open source design

00:45:09.971 --> 00:45:13.330
for the Secure Enclave
and ideally we

00:45:13.331 --> 00:45:16.240
would like to have formal
verification for the security

00:45:16.241 --> 00:45:19.690
and correctness of
the Secure Enclave,

00:45:19.691 --> 00:45:23.080
and also we need to
ensure secure supply to

00:45:23.081 --> 00:45:25.525
management to ensure the security

00:45:25.526 --> 00:45:28.190
of the manufacturing process.

00:45:28.500 --> 00:45:30.940
It's very important to have

00:45:30.941 --> 00:45:33.100
an open source and
Secure Enclave design.

00:45:33.101 --> 00:45:34.450
Currently none of

00:45:34.451 --> 00:45:39.114
the commercial Secure
Enclave TEE solutions

00:45:39.115 --> 00:45:41.275
are open to the public.

00:45:41.276 --> 00:45:43.615
Hence, as the public,

00:45:43.616 --> 00:45:46.120
we actually cannot
analyze or verify a

00:45:46.121 --> 00:45:49.165
hardware vendors
design in the setting,

00:45:49.166 --> 00:45:50.590
and also currently there's

00:45:50.591 --> 00:45:52.360
no industry agreements
and what is

00:45:52.361 --> 00:45:57.565
the right solution for
building Secure Enclave.

00:45:57.566 --> 00:46:00.895
With the open source
Secure Enclave design,

00:46:00.896 --> 00:46:02.980
it's to provides
the transparency that's

00:46:02.981 --> 00:46:05.950
needed for the whole
community to, together,

00:46:05.951 --> 00:46:10.900
analyze and verify the security
and the correctness of

00:46:10.901 --> 00:46:12.880
the Secure Enclave and to enable

00:46:12.881 --> 00:46:15.805
high assurance and open source

00:46:15.806 --> 00:46:19.100
help build a whole community.

00:46:19.500 --> 00:46:23.080
So, there has been
greater efforts on

00:46:23.081 --> 00:46:26.365
building open-source
secure hardware.

00:46:26.366 --> 00:46:31.915
One example is Sanctum from MIT,

00:46:31.916 --> 00:46:36.805
which builds a secure processor
design using RISC-V ISA,

00:46:36.806 --> 00:46:39.925
and it's designed to be

00:46:39.926 --> 00:46:41.050
able to defend against

00:46:41.051 --> 00:46:43.585
the cash-based
side-channel attacks.

00:46:43.586 --> 00:46:46.690
So, extending
Sanctum, we now have

00:46:46.691 --> 00:46:49.450
a new project called
the Keystone in

00:46:49.451 --> 00:46:52.525
collaboration between Berkeley
and MIT and there are

00:46:52.526 --> 00:46:57.505
other institutions who will
be joining the team as well.

00:46:57.506 --> 00:47:00.190
So the Keystone Enclave is

00:47:00.191 --> 00:47:05.380
an open source Secure Enclave
on top of RISC-V,

00:47:05.381 --> 00:47:07.300
and in particular leveraging

00:47:07.301 --> 00:47:10.660
the existing physical
hardware capabilities

00:47:10.661 --> 00:47:14.185
already available in
today's RISC-V tips.

00:47:14.186 --> 00:47:17.275
The goal is to provide
a strong memory isolation,

00:47:17.276 --> 00:47:23.125
we want it to be simple
and portable and provides

00:47:23.126 --> 00:47:27.415
a remote attestation capabilities
and fully open-source.

00:47:27.416 --> 00:47:29.920
There are a number of goals for

00:47:29.921 --> 00:47:34.150
the Keystone design that are

00:47:34.151 --> 00:47:37.915
important components to
achieve overall the goal

00:47:37.916 --> 00:47:39.190
of building a truly

00:47:39.191 --> 00:47:42.800
trustworthy open
sources Secure Enclave.

00:47:44.220 --> 00:47:51.850
I won't go into
individual goals in detail,

00:47:51.851 --> 00:47:54.160
and then certainly will talk more

00:47:54.161 --> 00:47:57.830
about the side channel aspects.

00:48:02.160 --> 00:48:05.020
So Keystone leverages

00:48:05.021 --> 00:48:08.845
the existing physical
hardware capabilities

00:48:08.846 --> 00:48:11.155
into these RISC-V tips,

00:48:11.156 --> 00:48:15.655
and to build the
open-source Secure Enclave.

00:48:15.656 --> 00:48:18.235
By using this Secure Enclave,

00:48:18.236 --> 00:48:21.590
essentially it helps to

00:48:21.600 --> 00:48:26.365
enable the system to separate
the privilege and trusts.

00:48:26.366 --> 00:48:28.570
So, in particularly in this case

00:48:28.571 --> 00:48:29.950
we actually have three modes;

00:48:29.951 --> 00:48:33.310
the user mode, the supervisor
mode and the machine modes.

00:48:33.311 --> 00:48:35.350
The enclave and also

00:48:35.351 --> 00:48:39.265
the components that enable
the Secure Enclave,

00:48:39.266 --> 00:48:42.370
are run at the machine
modes level.

00:48:42.371 --> 00:48:43.915
Whereas the operating system

00:48:43.916 --> 00:48:48.100
are running in
the supervisor mode

00:48:48.101 --> 00:48:55.450
and other user processes are
running under the user mode.

00:48:55.451 --> 00:48:58.255
One of the key components for

00:48:58.256 --> 00:49:01.765
Keystone as the security monitor,

00:49:01.766 --> 00:49:07.915
which essentially helps
provides the memory isolation.

00:49:07.916 --> 00:49:12.850
In particular, we enable
the physical memory protection

00:49:12.851 --> 00:49:14.680
with the machine modes and

00:49:14.681 --> 00:49:18.890
the virtual memory oscillation
with the S-mode.

00:49:20.160 --> 00:49:24.400
The Keystone in
particularly relies on

00:49:24.401 --> 00:49:28.270
the standard RISC-V primitives.

00:49:28.271 --> 00:49:29.740
In particular there are

00:49:29.741 --> 00:49:31.885
two main primitives
that we rely on.

00:49:31.886 --> 00:49:34.149
One is the Physical
Memory Protection,

00:49:34.150 --> 00:49:39.490
PMP, and the Trap
Virtual Memory, TVM.

00:49:39.491 --> 00:49:43.000
Due to time limit, I wont

00:49:43.001 --> 00:49:46.030
have time to go into
the details of all these,

00:49:46.031 --> 00:49:52.240
but these hardware capabilities
make it actually much

00:49:52.241 --> 00:49:55.750
easier to provide
the memory isolation

00:49:55.751 --> 00:49:59.395
for the Secure Enclave.

00:49:59.396 --> 00:50:01.150
As a result,

00:50:01.151 --> 00:50:04.270
the Keystone design for

00:50:04.271 --> 00:50:06.910
Secure Enclave actually
is much simpler than,

00:50:06.911 --> 00:50:12.620
for example, SGX and also
the Sanctum as well.

00:50:13.320 --> 00:50:16.660
So, this is the Keystone
architecture, again,

00:50:16.661 --> 00:50:21.140
I won't have time to
go into the details.

00:50:22.440 --> 00:50:28.405
So, I will actually
skip these, and again,

00:50:28.406 --> 00:50:32.635
because of the fact that
the Keystone leverages

00:50:32.636 --> 00:50:35.755
existing hardware capabilities in

00:50:35.756 --> 00:50:40.615
RISC-V for the memory amendments,

00:50:40.616 --> 00:50:46.015
it can provides
much more flexibility

00:50:46.016 --> 00:50:48.325
in terms of the type of

00:50:48.326 --> 00:50:50.590
enclave that they can enable in

00:50:50.591 --> 00:50:52.750
the sense that
the enclave size can be

00:50:52.751 --> 00:50:55.090
dynamic and where
the enclave can be

00:50:55.091 --> 00:50:58.580
located is dynamic as well.

00:51:03.420 --> 00:51:07.210
So due to time limits
I think I have

00:51:07.211 --> 00:51:11.050
to skip these details,

00:51:11.051 --> 00:51:13.795
how we actually use the PBM

00:51:13.796 --> 00:51:18.110
and TVM to enable
the memory isolation.

00:51:19.920 --> 00:51:24.760
Also Keystone plans to use

00:51:24.761 --> 00:51:32.365
other paths to help with
the key management as well.

00:51:32.366 --> 00:51:41.635
So, as a collaboration
between Berkeley and MIT,

00:51:41.636 --> 00:51:45.970
so we are working hard building

00:51:45.971 --> 00:51:48.655
the Keystone Secure Enclave,

00:51:48.656 --> 00:51:53.110
and we have a timeline that
we hope that in the fall,

00:51:53.111 --> 00:51:54.220
we actually will have

00:51:54.221 --> 00:51:57.100
a first release after
Secure Enclave,

00:51:57.101 --> 00:52:00.250
where it won't be full featured.

00:52:00.251 --> 00:52:04.240
In particular the first release
will contain some of

00:52:04.241 --> 00:52:08.410
the key capabilities for

00:52:08.411 --> 00:52:12.340
memory isolation and
some capabilities

00:52:12.341 --> 00:52:14.440
from the secure boot,

00:52:14.441 --> 00:52:18.610
remote attestation, and to
secure key provisioning.

00:52:19.950 --> 00:52:23.320
So, we plan to actually deploy

00:52:23.321 --> 00:52:26.440
the efforts to the Amazon AWS,

00:52:26.441 --> 00:52:30.580
using the FPGA as a first
deployments and later we plan

00:52:30.581 --> 00:52:36.590
to have it deployed on
the NEO HiFive unleashed chip.

00:52:38.910 --> 00:52:42.290
So using such capability for

00:52:42.291 --> 00:52:45.750
confidentiality preserving
smart contract execution.

00:52:45.751 --> 00:52:51.030
Again, we can enable privacy,
preserving smart contracts.

00:52:51.031 --> 00:52:53.730
They always use
Blockchain platform,

00:52:53.731 --> 00:52:57.210
and using these
overall capabilities,

00:52:57.211 --> 00:52:59.654
we hope to truly enable

00:52:59.655 --> 00:53:03.185
a blockchain of intelligent
smart contracts.

00:53:03.186 --> 00:53:05.800
Here, let me just give
you one example of

00:53:05.801 --> 00:53:08.710
a simple application that
we have been building and

00:53:08.711 --> 00:53:13.435
together as collaboration
with researchers and doctors

00:53:13.436 --> 00:53:20.170
from ETH and Stanford
for an app called Kara,

00:53:20.171 --> 00:53:21.460
a privacy preserving

00:53:21.461 --> 00:53:24.590
tokenized data market
for medical data.

00:53:24.720 --> 00:53:27.070
So as we all know,

00:53:27.071 --> 00:53:29.290
medical data is often
locked in data silos,

00:53:29.291 --> 00:53:31.390
so the goal here is
that we want to be

00:53:31.391 --> 00:53:33.490
able to incentivize doctors and

00:53:33.491 --> 00:53:35.470
patients to share data

00:53:35.471 --> 00:53:39.774
and to be able to
improve medical research

00:53:39.775 --> 00:53:44.470
and by leveraging the capabilities
of privacy preserving

00:53:44.471 --> 00:53:48.880
smart contracts on top of
the Oasis Blockchain platform.

00:53:48.881 --> 00:53:51.900
So in this case,
users and patients,

00:53:51.901 --> 00:53:54.255
can upload to
their medical data in

00:53:54.256 --> 00:53:58.000
encrypted form to the Oasis
blockchain platform and

00:53:58.001 --> 00:54:00.775
then the medical researchers
can essentially build

00:54:00.776 --> 00:54:02.785
privacy preserving
smart contracts

00:54:02.786 --> 00:54:04.255
and within this smart contracts,

00:54:04.256 --> 00:54:07.285
the researchers can include

00:54:07.286 --> 00:54:09.535
Machine Learning, model training,

00:54:09.536 --> 00:54:11.710
and model serving codes.

00:54:11.711 --> 00:54:14.620
Also the privacy
preserving smart contracts

00:54:14.621 --> 00:54:16.975
can specify terms of
use, for example,

00:54:16.976 --> 00:54:20.889
how users may get the
compensation for contributing

00:54:20.890 --> 00:54:22.705
their data to the smart contracts

00:54:22.706 --> 00:54:25.315
and also as stating that,

00:54:25.316 --> 00:54:28.210
for example, the data
will only be used for

00:54:28.211 --> 00:54:30.610
training Machine Learning model

00:54:30.611 --> 00:54:32.575
inside the smart contract.

00:54:32.576 --> 00:54:35.965
As users agree with
the terms of use,

00:54:35.966 --> 00:54:38.425
users medical data
can be contributed

00:54:38.426 --> 00:54:40.960
to the smart contracts.

00:54:40.961 --> 00:54:43.435
As the smart contract
collects enough data,

00:54:43.436 --> 00:54:45.310
it can then run

00:54:45.311 --> 00:54:47.650
the Machine Learning
model training codes to

00:54:47.651 --> 00:54:50.350
train the machinery models
and then in the end,

00:54:50.351 --> 00:54:52.315
the medical researchers can then

00:54:52.316 --> 00:54:55.720
evaluate the trained
Machine Learning models.

00:54:55.721 --> 00:54:57.910
So in this way,

00:54:57.911 --> 00:55:00.610
it helps to significantly

00:55:00.611 --> 00:55:03.520
reduce the friction for
medical researchers and

00:55:03.521 --> 00:55:07.030
pharmaceutical companies
to get access to data for

00:55:07.031 --> 00:55:09.610
training Machine
Learning models to

00:55:09.611 --> 00:55:13.700
find better cures for diseases.

00:55:14.880 --> 00:55:22.490
Then we have some upcoming paper
at our conferences.

00:55:23.550 --> 00:55:27.250
To summarize for
the technologies that have

00:55:27.251 --> 00:55:31.540
been discussing and
so Oasis Labs has

00:55:31.541 --> 00:55:34.540
just recently launched and

00:55:34.541 --> 00:55:38.665
also we have launched
our private Testnet where

00:55:38.666 --> 00:55:41.890
we encourage and invite

00:55:41.891 --> 00:55:44.095
our patient developers to join

00:55:44.096 --> 00:55:46.195
our private testnets to build

00:55:46.196 --> 00:55:48.400
applications on top
of the platform.

00:55:48.401 --> 00:55:49.540
So the current example I

00:55:49.541 --> 00:55:51.790
mentioned is actually
a collaboration

00:55:51.791 --> 00:55:54.399
with some of the app developers

00:55:54.400 --> 00:55:57.200
to build on top of our platform.

00:55:57.630 --> 00:56:01.300
With all these technologies
and Oasis Labs,

00:56:01.301 --> 00:56:03.490
we hope to build a privacy first,

00:56:03.491 --> 00:56:07.570
high-performance cloud computing
platform on blockchain.

00:56:07.571 --> 00:56:10.090
All this work for Keystone

00:56:10.091 --> 00:56:13.644
is a collaboration
between MIT and Berkeley,

00:56:13.645 --> 00:56:18.130
and we have a great team
as Oasis Labs.

00:56:18.131 --> 00:56:20.290
Our longer goal is
that we want to

00:56:20.291 --> 00:56:23.770
build a truly trustworthy,

00:56:23.771 --> 00:56:26.170
secure hardware that can

00:56:26.171 --> 00:56:30.400
enable all these
great applications.

00:56:30.401 --> 00:56:32.620
However, as I
mentioned in the talk,

00:56:32.621 --> 00:56:35.260
there are many challenges
that I actually

00:56:35.261 --> 00:56:38.275
didn't have time to go
into all the details.

00:56:38.276 --> 00:56:39.790
So more researchers are needed

00:56:39.791 --> 00:56:41.485
for research and development,

00:56:41.486 --> 00:56:43.840
and it requires
a community efforts.

00:56:43.841 --> 00:56:48.145
So let's tackle the big
challenges together. Thank you.

00:56:48.146 --> 00:56:52.900
&gt;&gt; Thank you. We have

00:56:52.901 --> 00:56:56.060
time for a couple
of questions. Yes?

00:56:57.800 --> 00:57:01.500
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]. How did you plan
to tackle the nontechnical issues?

00:57:01.501 --> 00:57:03.370
For example, you
talked about verifying

00:57:03.371 --> 00:57:04.500
the supply chain for

00:57:04.501 --> 00:57:08.060
building these chips
and somebody may

00:57:08.061 --> 00:57:10.460
trust the US regulated company

00:57:10.461 --> 00:57:13.780
like Intel for example
or whatever and somebody

00:57:13.781 --> 00:57:16.740
may prefer a EU regulated company

00:57:16.741 --> 00:57:18.750
doing that and somebody
a Chinese regulate

00:57:18.751 --> 00:57:21.310
company like how do we
agree on these things

00:57:21.311 --> 00:57:24.340
that don't have to do
much with technology?

00:57:24.341 --> 00:57:25.810
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great question.

00:57:25.811 --> 00:57:29.560
So, that's actually
another reason

00:57:29.561 --> 00:57:32.305
why we want to have

00:57:32.306 --> 00:57:35.290
open-source secure enclave
so then in this way,

00:57:35.291 --> 00:57:38.605
we don't have to rely
on any single party.

00:57:38.606 --> 00:57:40.480
So any hardware manufacturer

00:57:40.481 --> 00:57:42.935
can then go manufacturers and

00:57:42.936 --> 00:57:44.805
different users can also choose

00:57:44.806 --> 00:57:46.560
which hardware manufacturers that

00:57:46.561 --> 00:57:48.185
they trust more than others.

00:57:48.186 --> 00:57:50.305
Also, I do strongly believe that

00:57:50.306 --> 00:57:52.990
while the whole community
will actually or

00:57:52.991 --> 00:57:55.615
they have a lot of
experience in many of

00:57:55.616 --> 00:57:58.585
these other aspects of putting

00:57:58.586 --> 00:58:02.545
secure enclave but the
secure supply chain management

00:58:02.546 --> 00:58:04.990
is the one area where
as the whole community,

00:58:04.991 --> 00:58:08.260
we actually haven't
really tackle it head on.

00:58:08.261 --> 00:58:09.595
We don't actually have a lots of

00:58:09.596 --> 00:58:12.310
experiencing but it's
a very important area.

00:58:12.311 --> 00:58:14.290
So, we hope that with

00:58:14.291 --> 00:58:17.830
the Keystone projects and we're
actually helping to build

00:58:17.831 --> 00:58:19.275
a community together with

00:58:19.276 --> 00:58:21.490
different institutions and

00:58:21.491 --> 00:58:24.085
the hardware manufacturers
as a whole community,

00:58:24.086 --> 00:58:26.440
that actually can be an area of

00:58:26.441 --> 00:58:31.930
focus as a next step
in the near future,

00:58:31.931 --> 00:58:35.095
and that's where we can put
the proper processes and

00:58:35.096 --> 00:58:38.170
also various verification
procedures to help

00:58:38.171 --> 00:58:41.440
us to make progress

00:58:41.441 --> 00:58:44.590
towards this goal of
secure supply chain management.

00:58:44.591 --> 00:58:46.330
&gt;&gt; All right. Let's
thank Dawn again.

00:58:46.331 --> 00:58:51.430
Thank you. Our next speaker

00:58:51.431 --> 00:58:54.840
is Professor Srini
Devadas from MIT.

00:58:54.841 --> 00:58:56.385
He's a Webster Professor of

00:58:56.386 --> 00:59:00.300
Electrical Engineering and
Computer Science at MIT.

00:59:00.301 --> 00:59:03.230
His interests are also
broad in Computer Security,

00:59:03.231 --> 00:59:06.290
Computer Architecture,
and The Applied crypto.

00:59:06.291 --> 00:59:10.340
Srini received the 2017 IEEE

00:59:10.341 --> 00:59:15.645
W. Wallace amygdala
Award and the 2018 IEEE,

00:59:15.646 --> 00:59:19.965
Charles A Technical
Achievement Award,

00:59:19.966 --> 00:59:23.210
for his research in
secure hardware.

00:59:23.211 --> 00:59:28.970
He also received the MIT's
highest teaching honor,

00:59:28.971 --> 00:59:32.180
the Everett Moore Baker
Memorial Award and the

00:59:32.181 --> 00:59:35.105
Bose Award for
Excellence in teaching.

00:59:35.106 --> 00:59:37.885
So thank you very much
and hand over to you.

00:59:37.886 --> 00:59:40.540
&gt;&gt; Thanks for the kind
introduction, Manuel.

00:59:40.541 --> 00:59:42.850
I want to give you folks a sense

00:59:42.851 --> 00:59:45.140
of what is going to take to build

00:59:45.141 --> 00:59:47.420
secure computer
architectures that can do

00:59:47.421 --> 00:59:50.865
the things that the previous
two speakers want to do,

00:59:50.866 --> 00:59:53.580
the lightweight
secure enclaves and so on,

00:59:53.581 --> 00:59:56.035
and it's fairly challenging.

00:59:56.036 --> 00:59:58.650
They've been many attacks

00:59:58.651 --> 01:00:00.450
exploiting side channels but not

01:00:00.451 --> 01:00:02.645
just side-channel attacks but

01:00:02.646 --> 01:00:05.400
attacks exploiting
out-of-order execution,

01:00:05.401 --> 01:00:07.340
speculative execution
that have come

01:00:07.341 --> 01:00:10.520
out and I want to give
you a sense of how

01:00:10.521 --> 01:00:13.150
these attacks work because
we need to understand how

01:00:13.151 --> 01:00:14.330
these attacks work in order

01:00:14.331 --> 01:00:16.420
to actually defend against them.

01:00:16.421 --> 01:00:19.260
So, we've been working
on defenses against

01:00:19.261 --> 01:00:22.630
the latest and most portent
of these attacks,

01:00:22.631 --> 01:00:24.065
I'll tell you
a little bit about that,

01:00:24.066 --> 01:00:27.440
and I'll close with talking
about secure enclaves.

01:00:27.441 --> 01:00:29.070
I'll be brief
because they've been

01:00:29.071 --> 01:00:33.170
covered quite nicely
by Mark and Dawn.

01:00:33.171 --> 01:00:35.685
But the stakes are
a little bit higher

01:00:35.686 --> 01:00:37.600
because in the case

01:00:37.601 --> 01:00:39.454
of just building
conventional processors,

01:00:39.455 --> 01:00:41.515
you get to trust system software,

01:00:41.516 --> 01:00:43.800
you get to trust
the operating system,

01:00:43.801 --> 01:00:46.420
and you can make it
do a work for you

01:00:46.421 --> 01:00:50.310
that corresponds to handling
sensitive data but you

01:00:50.311 --> 01:00:51.450
can't do that when you have a

01:00:51.451 --> 01:00:53.010
secure enclave if
you want to minimize

01:00:53.011 --> 01:00:54.840
the trusted computing base
and you want to

01:00:54.841 --> 01:00:56.860
shrink it down by
whatever it is two,

01:00:56.861 --> 01:00:59.080
three orders of magnitude
from tens of millions of

01:00:59.081 --> 01:01:01.745
lines of code down to
thousands of lines of code.

01:01:01.746 --> 01:01:04.905
So, I'll say a little bit
about that then.

01:01:04.906 --> 01:01:07.460
So, let me just start.

01:01:07.461 --> 01:01:10.370
So you're probably thinking,
"What is this picture?"

01:01:10.371 --> 01:01:12.530
Well, I had this
really pretty picture

01:01:12.531 --> 01:01:15.730
of the described
compartmentalization and

01:01:15.731 --> 01:01:18.770
secure containers in processors

01:01:18.771 --> 01:01:21.900
and I realized I probably
illegally downloaded it.

01:01:21.901 --> 01:01:23.420
So this is a picture of

01:01:23.421 --> 01:01:28.070
my toolbox and so I
own this picture.

01:01:28.071 --> 01:01:31.040
It essentially gives
you a sense more

01:01:31.041 --> 01:01:34.495
seriously about what
architectures have to do.

01:01:34.496 --> 01:01:37.370
Architectures have to isolate
processes from each other.

01:01:37.371 --> 01:01:40.340
This is fundamental
to multiprocessing.

01:01:40.341 --> 01:01:41.950
It's fundamental to time sharing.

01:01:41.951 --> 01:01:44.840
It's been with us for 50 years.

01:01:44.841 --> 01:01:46.865
If this is violated,

01:01:46.866 --> 01:01:48.070
if architectural isolation is

01:01:48.071 --> 01:01:49.495
violated, we really have nothing.

01:01:49.496 --> 01:01:50.630
I mean we have no security,

01:01:50.631 --> 01:01:53.070
we have no privacy, you
don't have integrity either.

01:01:53.071 --> 01:01:55.520
So it's important to maintain

01:01:55.521 --> 01:02:00.690
architectural isolation
and over the years,

01:02:00.691 --> 01:02:03.270
computer manufacturers have used

01:02:03.271 --> 01:02:06.280
many different performance
optimizations in order to

01:02:06.281 --> 01:02:11.840
improve your IPC instructions
per cycle et cetera,

01:02:11.841 --> 01:02:16.070
and it turns out
these operations were done

01:02:16.071 --> 01:02:18.370
largely at the
microarchitectural level

01:02:18.371 --> 01:02:20.840
and they were hidden
from the ISA,

01:02:20.841 --> 01:02:24.600
the Instruction Set
Architecture, and in particular,

01:02:24.601 --> 01:02:28.520
these microarchitectural
optimizations have been shown

01:02:28.521 --> 01:02:30.460
to leak privacy and

01:02:30.461 --> 01:02:32.780
potentially influence
correctness as well.

01:02:32.781 --> 01:02:34.850
So, let me say a little bit

01:02:34.851 --> 01:02:38.180
about the latest of these attacks

01:02:38.181 --> 01:02:39.250
corresponding to spectrum

01:02:39.251 --> 01:02:40.660
meltdown that obviously received

01:02:40.661 --> 01:02:43.855
a lot of press but
very briefly here,

01:02:43.856 --> 01:02:46.330
what one can do if you want

01:02:46.331 --> 01:02:48.590
to just execute
a sequential program

01:02:48.591 --> 01:02:50.715
is obviously go
from instruction I

01:02:50.716 --> 01:02:53.050
to I plus one to I
plus two et cetera,

01:02:53.051 --> 01:02:54.800
and every once in a while
you're obviously going to have

01:02:54.801 --> 01:02:58.230
a branch and that's
corresponds to control flow.

01:02:58.231 --> 01:03:00.360
You could certainly wait until

01:03:00.361 --> 01:03:03.220
the branch has been resolved
so you know that you

01:03:03.221 --> 01:03:05.920
want to go in this left direction

01:03:05.921 --> 01:03:07.950
but if in fact it

01:03:07.951 --> 01:03:10.070
takes a long time for
the branch to be resolved,

01:03:10.071 --> 01:03:11.090
because you have to go look at

01:03:11.091 --> 01:03:12.970
the memory location for example,

01:03:12.971 --> 01:03:16.810
then you might in fact
decide to speculate.

01:03:16.811 --> 01:03:19.365
So, you decide that

01:03:19.366 --> 01:03:21.920
you probably going to go
left and you decide to

01:03:21.921 --> 01:03:23.590
take the left fork and you start

01:03:23.591 --> 01:03:25.620
computing J and J plus one even

01:03:25.621 --> 01:03:28.030
before you finish
up on instruction

01:03:28.031 --> 01:03:31.340
I and if you speculate correctly,

01:03:31.341 --> 01:03:33.775
then everything is great and
you've gained performance.

01:03:33.776 --> 01:03:35.300
But if you miscalculated and

01:03:35.301 --> 01:03:37.340
you've decided that
what you really

01:03:37.341 --> 01:03:41.555
should be doing is to
go right, I should say,

01:03:41.556 --> 01:03:44.630
you decided to go right but
in fact you had to go left,

01:03:44.631 --> 01:03:46.820
then you've executed K
and potentially K plus

01:03:46.821 --> 01:03:49.170
one and you have to roll back

01:03:49.171 --> 01:03:51.205
and you've done some things

01:03:51.206 --> 01:03:53.560
that can clearly be cleaned
up from a standpoint of

01:03:53.561 --> 01:03:55.090
architectural state and there's

01:03:55.091 --> 01:03:56.250
no question that processors

01:03:56.251 --> 01:03:59.080
today definitely do this cleanup.

01:03:59.081 --> 01:04:01.020
But unfortunately,
what happens is that

01:04:01.021 --> 01:04:02.920
while you're executing
K and K plus

01:04:02.921 --> 01:04:05.180
one in this incorrect way

01:04:05.181 --> 01:04:07.420
or should I say in
a mispeculated way,

01:04:07.421 --> 01:04:09.415
you may have changed the
micro architectural state

01:04:09.416 --> 01:04:10.955
and that could leak information.

01:04:10.956 --> 01:04:14.850
So, this notion of speculation

01:04:14.851 --> 01:04:17.610
was considered just a
performance optimization

01:04:17.611 --> 01:04:20.465
but it has serious
security implications.

01:04:20.466 --> 01:04:22.700
Essentially, the attacks
that have been proposed,

01:04:22.701 --> 01:04:24.045
there's just a wide
variety of them.

01:04:24.046 --> 01:04:26.680
I mean every conference
seems to have sessions now,

01:04:26.681 --> 01:04:28.895
architecture conferences
or new types of attacks,

01:04:28.896 --> 01:04:30.945
but they have two
characteristics in common.

01:04:30.946 --> 01:04:38.230
First, primarily, I
exploit cache tag state.

01:04:38.231 --> 01:04:40.080
So, you have caches
which are obviously

01:04:40.081 --> 01:04:41.190
important for performance and

01:04:41.191 --> 01:04:42.365
they've been around for a while,

01:04:42.366 --> 01:04:44.420
caches are micro
architectural state

01:04:44.421 --> 01:04:46.800
and you bring things
into the cache,

01:04:46.801 --> 01:04:48.380
when you execute it
for example the K

01:04:48.381 --> 01:04:50.105
and K plus one incorrectly,

01:04:50.106 --> 01:04:54.510
and that changes
the state of the cache.

01:04:54.511 --> 01:04:57.170
The cache state,
unfortunately, in speculation,

01:04:57.171 --> 01:05:00.960
is not rolled back in
the conventional processors,

01:05:00.961 --> 01:05:02.370
and because you don't do that,

01:05:02.371 --> 01:05:04.835
there's some side effects
to the misspeculation.

01:05:04.836 --> 01:05:06.360
The other thing
that's interesting is

01:05:06.361 --> 01:05:09.880
that you have
branch predictors and

01:05:09.881 --> 01:05:13.105
computers that correspond to

01:05:13.106 --> 01:05:16.285
deciding how to predict
which way to go,

01:05:16.286 --> 01:05:17.815
whether to go left or right.

01:05:17.816 --> 01:05:20.070
In this case, you
have a situation

01:05:20.071 --> 01:05:23.010
where the learning associated
with a branch predictor,

01:05:23.011 --> 01:05:24.510
I mean, these are not
deep learning engines,

01:05:24.511 --> 01:05:25.615
or at least not yet.

01:05:25.616 --> 01:05:28.530
But they definitely have

01:05:28.531 --> 01:05:33.380
capabilities associated with
history cables, et cetera.

01:05:33.381 --> 01:05:35.820
The inputs to
these branch predictors,

01:05:35.821 --> 01:05:37.270
that respond to learning

01:05:37.271 --> 01:05:39.340
which way to go for
a particular branch,

01:05:39.341 --> 01:05:41.355
may come from other processes.

01:05:41.356 --> 01:05:44.280
So, there's no isolation

01:05:44.281 --> 01:05:45.440
within the branch predictors on

01:05:45.441 --> 01:05:47.000
learning from your own process,

01:05:47.001 --> 01:05:48.660
and that is actually also being

01:05:48.661 --> 01:05:51.550
exploited in these attacks.

01:05:51.551 --> 01:05:53.755
So, this is really
a huge attack surface,

01:05:53.756 --> 01:05:55.470
and so this is really a picture

01:05:55.471 --> 01:05:57.470
that I won't spend
so much time on,

01:05:57.471 --> 01:06:00.695
but I leave you with spice
potentially everywhere,

01:06:00.696 --> 01:06:02.560
because you have processes

01:06:02.561 --> 01:06:04.215
that are sharing branch tables,

01:06:04.216 --> 01:06:06.825
processes that are
sharing caches,

01:06:06.826 --> 01:06:09.520
DRAM controllers,
there's timing channels,

01:06:09.521 --> 01:06:11.325
cache tags, and so
on and so forth.

01:06:11.326 --> 01:06:12.690
So, this is
a fairly exciting time

01:06:12.691 --> 01:06:13.895
in computer architecture,

01:06:13.896 --> 01:06:15.320
primarily because
we have to go back

01:06:15.321 --> 01:06:16.895
and rethink how we would

01:06:16.896 --> 01:06:21.110
do time-sharing in the context
of all of these spies.

01:06:21.111 --> 01:06:24.120
So, I want to give
you a sense of how

01:06:24.121 --> 01:06:26.610
to categorize
these different attacks.

01:06:26.611 --> 01:06:29.400
So, here's a little picture
that taxonomized

01:06:29.401 --> 01:06:31.240
attacks corresponding

01:06:31.241 --> 01:06:33.975
to both the new ones
and the old ones.

01:06:33.976 --> 01:06:36.275
So, the idea here is that

01:06:36.276 --> 01:06:38.350
you have a domain, you
have protection domains.

01:06:38.351 --> 01:06:39.895
That's really what
time-sharing is about.

01:06:39.896 --> 01:06:41.395
So, you have a domain
of a victim,

01:06:41.396 --> 01:06:42.730
the victim has some secret

01:06:42.731 --> 01:06:44.535
that the victim wants to touch,

01:06:44.536 --> 01:06:47.985
and use for encryption or maybe
it's just sensitive data,

01:06:47.986 --> 01:06:49.510
medical records, what have you,

01:06:49.511 --> 01:06:50.810
and then you have an attacker.

01:06:50.811 --> 01:06:52.280
The attacker is concomitantly

01:06:52.281 --> 01:06:54.130
using resources of the machine,

01:06:54.131 --> 01:06:56.290
it may simply be
a time-shared situation,

01:06:56.291 --> 01:06:58.280
you may only have a single core,

01:06:58.281 --> 01:07:00.555
but they also could
be multicores,

01:07:00.556 --> 01:07:02.280
and there could be
space sharing as well.

01:07:02.281 --> 01:07:04.355
So, these two domains are
supposed to be separate.

01:07:04.356 --> 01:07:05.400
You have two different domains

01:07:05.401 --> 01:07:07.760
corresponding to the victim
and the attacker.

01:07:07.761 --> 01:07:10.350
The first thing is that
there's these channels,

01:07:10.351 --> 01:07:11.420
these channels are these side

01:07:11.421 --> 01:07:13.350
channels that have to be created,

01:07:13.351 --> 01:07:14.570
and then any number of them,

01:07:14.571 --> 01:07:16.470
in a cache tag state, the most

01:07:16.471 --> 01:07:20.775
common in at least in
current day attacks,

01:07:20.776 --> 01:07:22.850
but there are others as well.

01:07:22.851 --> 01:07:26.055
What the attacker does is
it creates a receiver.

01:07:26.056 --> 01:07:28.000
So, this is really a
piece of code that can

01:07:28.001 --> 01:07:30.195
somehow exfiltrate
through this channel,

01:07:30.196 --> 01:07:32.230
the secret or some form
of the secret that

01:07:32.231 --> 01:07:34.810
isn't the victims
protection domain.

01:07:34.811 --> 01:07:37.615
There's also a transmitter
that's associated with

01:07:37.616 --> 01:07:39.230
a piece of code and

01:07:39.231 --> 01:07:40.960
I'll tell you a little bit
more about this in a second,

01:07:40.961 --> 01:07:42.125
that touches the secret.

01:07:42.126 --> 01:07:43.420
So, this transmitter is actually

01:07:43.421 --> 01:07:45.455
running in the victim's code.

01:07:45.456 --> 01:07:48.425
So, it has permissions
associated with that.

01:07:48.426 --> 01:07:50.860
So, the basic idea is that,

01:07:50.861 --> 01:07:53.800
if you create this transmitter

01:07:53.801 --> 01:07:55.485
and the receiver and
you have this channel,

01:07:55.486 --> 01:08:00.060
well, you've essentially
a broken privacy of the victim.

01:08:00.061 --> 01:08:02.060
So, if you look

01:08:02.061 --> 01:08:04.315
at the different attacks
that are out there,

01:08:04.316 --> 01:08:05.690
and so just to give
you a sense of why

01:08:05.691 --> 01:08:07.665
this taxonomy is broad and

01:08:07.666 --> 01:08:09.680
incorporates
both the recent attacks

01:08:09.681 --> 01:08:11.310
and attacks that
are 20 years old,

01:08:11.311 --> 01:08:15.050
let me give you a sense
of an age-old attack

01:08:15.051 --> 01:08:17.260
that was done about 20 years ago

01:08:17.261 --> 01:08:19.935
on the RSA encryption algorithm.

01:08:19.936 --> 01:08:22.120
Where it has
conditional execution,

01:08:22.121 --> 01:08:24.750
depending on what
the bit of the key is,

01:08:24.751 --> 01:08:27.595
you either do a modulus
or not, roughly speaking,

01:08:27.596 --> 01:08:31.145
and you do a lot of computation
in one case going left,

01:08:31.146 --> 01:08:33.500
and you don't do very
much going right.

01:08:33.501 --> 01:08:37.110
This is a problem because this
is part of the algorithm,

01:08:37.111 --> 01:08:38.670
and this algorithm
is actually doing

01:08:38.671 --> 01:08:41.245
different things based on
the secret key or maybe has to,

01:08:41.246 --> 01:08:42.700
because, obviously the output

01:08:42.701 --> 01:08:44.260
has to depend on the secret key.

01:08:44.261 --> 01:08:46.895
But if you haven't written
the program in such a way,

01:08:46.896 --> 01:08:50.095
you could be in
a situation where,

01:08:50.096 --> 01:08:52.610
regardless of
speculative execution,

01:08:52.611 --> 01:08:54.320
you're just doing more work

01:08:54.321 --> 01:08:56.300
on the left-hand side
versus right hand side.

01:08:56.301 --> 01:08:58.320
The micro architectural
channel associated with

01:08:58.321 --> 01:09:01.215
the cache could change

01:09:01.216 --> 01:09:05.070
the execution
corresponding to what

01:09:05.071 --> 01:09:08.030
the execution would be
if the secret key bit

01:09:08.031 --> 01:09:09.540
were zero versus one.

01:09:09.541 --> 01:09:14.205
So, this change with respect
to going left or right is

01:09:14.206 --> 01:09:17.255
exfiltrated through
the cache tag channel

01:09:17.256 --> 01:09:18.620
based on caches and cache

01:09:18.621 --> 01:09:20.705
misses that can be
viewed by the attacker.

01:09:20.706 --> 01:09:22.340
So, you can get a sense of what

01:09:22.341 --> 01:09:24.795
the victim's cache hits
and cache misses are,

01:09:24.796 --> 01:09:27.140
based on what your cache
hits and cache misses are,

01:09:27.141 --> 01:09:28.455
if you shared the cache.

01:09:28.456 --> 01:09:30.390
So, this was an
attack that was done

01:09:30.391 --> 01:09:32.490
20 years ago and the fix
for this attack is,

01:09:32.491 --> 01:09:34.195
something that Mark mentioned,

01:09:34.196 --> 01:09:36.875
you can try to do
oblivious execution

01:09:36.876 --> 01:09:38.715
by balancing parts, et cetera.

01:09:38.716 --> 01:09:40.540
You can certainly
do this for RSA,

01:09:40.541 --> 01:09:43.150
but as you'll see,
cryptographic algorithms

01:09:43.151 --> 01:09:44.385
got a lot more complicated,

01:09:44.386 --> 01:09:48.870
and these attacks
are still valid in

01:09:48.871 --> 01:09:51.570
the case of anonymous
attestation schemes

01:09:51.571 --> 01:09:53.895
like SGX and I'll get to
that a little bit later.

01:09:53.896 --> 01:09:55.550
Now, you can take these attacks

01:09:55.551 --> 01:09:57.535
corresponding to
spector and meltdown,

01:09:57.536 --> 01:09:58.770
and you can view this in

01:09:58.771 --> 01:10:01.080
this taxonomy by thinking
about meltdown as being

01:10:01.081 --> 01:10:03.260
something where the attacker

01:10:03.261 --> 01:10:05.710
was able to write
this transmitter

01:10:05.711 --> 01:10:08.240
by essentially creating
a permissions check

01:10:08.241 --> 01:10:11.240
before accessing some data.

01:10:11.241 --> 01:10:13.055
Because of speculative execution,

01:10:13.056 --> 01:10:15.050
that data was actually
accessed and brought

01:10:15.051 --> 01:10:17.245
into the cache in
a particular corner case,

01:10:17.246 --> 01:10:20.945
before the permission stack
was actually validated.

01:10:20.946 --> 01:10:23.670
So, that was a corner case in

01:10:23.671 --> 01:10:25.610
the processor pipeline that

01:10:25.611 --> 01:10:27.370
corresponded to the meltdown bug.

01:10:27.371 --> 01:10:28.930
Spectre Is a broader class of

01:10:28.931 --> 01:10:30.560
attack where you can actually

01:10:30.561 --> 01:10:34.100
synthesize this transmitter
by looking at victim code.

01:10:34.101 --> 01:10:37.020
It's broader, it's also
harder to accomplish,

01:10:37.021 --> 01:10:39.550
but it's something that

01:10:39.551 --> 01:10:42.320
clearly has been
done at least in,

01:10:42.321 --> 01:10:44.790
let's say, in
the test tube level,

01:10:44.791 --> 01:10:46.605
if not in the wild.

01:10:46.606 --> 01:10:49.020
So, these things are things that

01:10:49.021 --> 01:10:50.980
just from a pure standpoint,

01:10:50.981 --> 01:10:52.920
are things that you
need to fix if you

01:10:52.921 --> 01:10:54.780
want architectural isolation the

01:10:54.781 --> 01:10:58.990
way it was promised
to us 60 years ago.

01:10:58.991 --> 01:11:02.415
So, how do you defend against
these types of attacks?

01:11:02.416 --> 01:11:04.440
I'll just focus on the cache
channel because that's

01:11:04.441 --> 01:11:06.750
the most popular channel
for exfiltration.

01:11:06.751 --> 01:11:09.055
It's a matter of
carefully designing

01:11:09.056 --> 01:11:13.405
processor caches in this case
to do perfect isolation.

01:11:13.406 --> 01:11:16.315
So, people have done
cache partitioning,

01:11:16.316 --> 01:11:20.370
this cache partitioning schemes
that are in processors,

01:11:20.371 --> 01:11:22.810
and in general to protect
against these attacks,

01:11:22.811 --> 01:11:24.110
the different schema
would be that you

01:11:24.111 --> 01:11:25.445
have the block one
of these steps.

01:11:25.446 --> 01:11:29.390
You got to break that pipe
there and we've focused on,

01:11:29.391 --> 01:11:32.340
in our research, focused
on blocking the channel.

01:11:32.341 --> 01:11:34.130
That seems like the easiest
thing to do but there's

01:11:34.131 --> 01:11:36.920
other ways of defending as well.

01:11:36.921 --> 01:11:40.655
So, Intel has Cache Allocation
Technology built-in.

01:11:40.656 --> 01:11:42.745
It's abbreviated CAT.

01:11:42.746 --> 01:11:44.665
Essentially, it's something that

01:11:44.666 --> 01:11:46.240
is a quality of
service mechanism,

01:11:46.241 --> 01:11:47.610
it wasn't meant for security,

01:11:47.611 --> 01:11:49.380
but it's a way of
making sure that

01:11:49.381 --> 01:11:52.180
a runaway process doesn't
trash the cache for you,

01:11:52.181 --> 01:11:54.620
and you still get
performance even

01:11:54.621 --> 01:11:57.005
though you're using
only part of the cache,

01:11:57.006 --> 01:11:59.195
you'd at least get
one part of the cache.

01:11:59.196 --> 01:12:01.180
Now, computer
architecture one-to-one,

01:12:01.181 --> 01:12:03.715
caches are made up
of sets and ways,

01:12:03.716 --> 01:12:06.530
you can think of the sets
as being horizontal rows,

01:12:06.531 --> 01:12:07.580
but what you see here are

01:12:07.581 --> 01:12:09.495
different colors
corresponding to the ways.

01:12:09.496 --> 01:12:10.995
Essentially, a way is

01:12:10.996 --> 01:12:12.660
something that says
if you have two ways,

01:12:12.661 --> 01:12:14.825
then you can either
go here or there

01:12:14.826 --> 01:12:18.510
in a particular set
and its associativity.

01:12:18.511 --> 01:12:22.370
So, you can partition the cache
using sets or using ways,

01:12:22.371 --> 01:12:25.010
CAT does it using ways.

01:12:25.011 --> 01:12:27.960
You can essentially say that

01:12:27.961 --> 01:12:30.655
the attacker cannot evict

01:12:30.656 --> 01:12:32.870
a piece of data from
the victim's way.

01:12:32.871 --> 01:12:34.160
That's essentially
what CAT does is,

01:12:34.161 --> 01:12:35.795
it's the quality of
service mechanism.

01:12:35.796 --> 01:12:39.110
I'm not quite enough
for isolation,

01:12:39.111 --> 01:12:41.725
for perfect isolation, for a
couple of different reasons.

01:12:41.726 --> 01:12:45.470
The first is that
shared addresses are visible.

01:12:45.471 --> 01:12:47.930
So, it's possible that you
have some shared libraries,

01:12:47.931 --> 01:12:51.100
and the victim and the attacker
could be sharing data,

01:12:51.101 --> 01:12:53.895
and that corresponds to

01:12:53.896 --> 01:12:56.170
the data that's in
the victim's way,

01:12:56.171 --> 01:12:58.495
for example, because
the victim access first.

01:12:58.496 --> 01:12:59.950
So, it's possible that

01:12:59.951 --> 01:13:04.480
that won't get evicted but
you can see the cache,

01:13:04.481 --> 01:13:07.375
it's associated with
the shared addresses.

01:13:07.376 --> 01:13:10.945
Then, the other part of, it
is a little bit more subtle,

01:13:10.946 --> 01:13:12.610
is that you have
replacement policies

01:13:12.611 --> 01:13:14.140
associated with these caches.

01:13:14.141 --> 01:13:16.220
LRU is the policy

01:13:16.221 --> 01:13:19.880
that you learn about in
computer architecture 101,

01:13:19.881 --> 01:13:21.610
recently used but
nobody builds LRU,

01:13:21.611 --> 01:13:23.835
at least not perfect LRU,
they're approximated.

01:13:23.836 --> 01:13:25.820
It turns out that
replacement metadata

01:13:25.821 --> 01:13:27.490
can leak information even

01:13:27.491 --> 01:13:28.900
though you had isolation in

01:13:28.901 --> 01:13:31.455
the waste or you had
isolation in the sets.

01:13:31.456 --> 01:13:34.155
So, I'm not going to go
into the details here,

01:13:34.156 --> 01:13:36.525
but roughly speaking, the reason,

01:13:36.526 --> 01:13:39.544
the first reason that
CAT leaks information,

01:13:39.545 --> 01:13:41.645
and it doesn't protect you,

01:13:41.646 --> 01:13:43.310
is simply that cache hits

01:13:43.311 --> 01:13:45.020
are not isolated across domains.

01:13:45.021 --> 01:13:47.440
So, in the shared address case,

01:13:47.441 --> 01:13:49.785
if an attacker brought

01:13:49.786 --> 01:13:52.745
a piece of shared data
into its way,

01:13:52.746 --> 01:13:54.610
the victim will hit on it.

01:13:54.611 --> 01:13:56.040
If the attacker didn't,

01:13:56.041 --> 01:13:57.275
then the victim would miss on it,

01:13:57.276 --> 01:13:59.295
and so you get
some information from that.

01:13:59.296 --> 01:14:02.780
Then, because you use
approximate LRU schemes,

01:14:02.781 --> 01:14:04.360
even though you have
this partitioning

01:14:04.361 --> 01:14:05.845
of blue and red over here,

01:14:05.846 --> 01:14:09.430
the first three columns here

01:14:09.431 --> 01:14:13.060
or the slots correspond
to one domain,

01:14:13.061 --> 01:14:15.155
the red one because one
to a different domain.

01:14:15.156 --> 01:14:16.815
You can think that
this isolation,

01:14:16.816 --> 01:14:19.500
but if you have
an approximate LRU scheme,

01:14:19.501 --> 01:14:21.900
a PLRU or Pseudo-LRU
which is used

01:14:21.901 --> 01:14:25.075
quite commonly in
various processors,

01:14:25.076 --> 01:14:27.405
it's possible that you can sense

01:14:27.406 --> 01:14:30.195
a difference in what was
evicted from the victim

01:14:30.196 --> 01:14:32.110
even though you had
this isolation because of

01:14:32.111 --> 01:14:36.625
the replacement policy
having imperfect isolation.

01:14:36.626 --> 01:14:40.650
So, CAT doesn't protect you
and so we had to improve it,

01:14:40.651 --> 01:14:43.410
and so we said well,
we'll call it DAWG.

01:14:43.411 --> 01:14:48.340
So, this is just an abbreviation,

01:14:48.341 --> 01:14:51.195
Dynamically Allocated Way Guards.

01:14:51.196 --> 01:14:53.120
It's a way of taking

01:14:53.121 --> 01:14:55.200
the CAT mechanism
and fixing these

01:14:55.201 --> 01:14:58.010
two that I've told you about.

01:14:58.011 --> 01:15:00.560
Just briefly, I just
have one on it,

01:15:00.561 --> 01:15:02.790
you have to track
global protection domains.

01:15:02.791 --> 01:15:04.220
You have to be very careful about

01:15:04.221 --> 01:15:05.350
what goes into the cache,

01:15:05.351 --> 01:15:07.160
and what gets evicted
from the cache,

01:15:07.161 --> 01:15:10.665
and you can do this
by essentially

01:15:10.666 --> 01:15:12.250
making sure that cache tags or

01:15:12.251 --> 01:15:14.500
replacement metadata don't
interfere with each other.

01:15:14.501 --> 01:15:16.200
It's a fairly simple change.

01:15:16.201 --> 01:15:18.270
Hardware manufacturers
will tell you that this is

01:15:18.271 --> 01:15:20.505
an invasive change
to the hardware,

01:15:20.506 --> 01:15:22.290
because you're messing with

01:15:22.291 --> 01:15:24.085
the cache hash
function, et cetera.

01:15:24.086 --> 01:15:26.400
But I'm a proponent

01:15:26.401 --> 01:15:28.440
of open source and
clean slate design,

01:15:28.441 --> 01:15:30.180
so for us, it's easy.

01:15:30.181 --> 01:15:32.380
But it's not something
that can be done.

01:15:32.381 --> 01:15:35.135
Any change in hardware obviously
takes years to deploy.

01:15:35.136 --> 01:15:36.710
But this is not something

01:15:36.711 --> 01:15:38.620
that would be as
invasive, I would say,

01:15:38.621 --> 01:15:40.250
as changing the
processor pipeline and

01:15:40.251 --> 01:15:42.480
changing the number of
stages for example.

01:15:42.481 --> 01:15:46.095
So, roughly speaking, you have

01:15:46.096 --> 01:15:48.290
additional data
associated with what gets

01:15:48.291 --> 01:15:51.145
evicted and what gets
pulled into the cache.

01:15:51.146 --> 01:15:53.350
Also, the replacement policy,

01:15:53.351 --> 01:15:55.500
perfect LRU does
not have a problem,

01:15:55.501 --> 01:15:57.395
random replacement
doesn't have a problem,

01:15:57.396 --> 01:15:59.965
but if you use
an approximate LRU scheme,

01:15:59.966 --> 01:16:01.365
you have to do
a little bit more work

01:16:01.366 --> 01:16:03.850
to ensure perfect isolation.

01:16:03.851 --> 01:16:06.550
So, now you'd say "Okay,

01:16:06.551 --> 01:16:07.825
this was the cash tag channel."

01:16:07.826 --> 01:16:09.265
I'll just leave you with

01:16:09.266 --> 01:16:11.640
this other micro
architectural state including

01:16:11.641 --> 01:16:14.455
branch predictors state
that needs to be isolated,

01:16:14.456 --> 01:16:15.675
if there were attacks,

01:16:15.676 --> 01:16:17.640
there were potent enough to

01:16:17.641 --> 01:16:19.120
exfiltrate through
those channels,

01:16:19.121 --> 01:16:21.230
and you could use this type
of technique over there.

01:16:21.231 --> 01:16:23.710
Again, you have to look
very carefully at what you

01:16:23.711 --> 01:16:26.570
bring how you modify the
micro architectural state.

01:16:26.571 --> 01:16:29.135
There is a little bit
of a complication.

01:16:29.136 --> 01:16:33.440
The unfortunate part of
the dog scheme that we

01:16:33.441 --> 01:16:35.440
derived is that you're

01:16:35.441 --> 01:16:38.110
disallowing hits across
protection domain.

01:16:38.111 --> 01:16:39.705
So, if you have
some shared addresses,

01:16:39.706 --> 01:16:41.720
then it's possible that
these shared addresses

01:16:41.721 --> 01:16:43.805
will be replicated across ways,

01:16:43.806 --> 01:16:46.120
so the same address
in two two ways of

01:16:46.121 --> 01:16:48.625
the cache and this is
okay for read only data,

01:16:48.626 --> 01:16:50.980
but obviously it
would be a problem if

01:16:50.981 --> 01:16:53.730
you guess wrong and the data

01:16:53.731 --> 01:16:56.410
was writable and so you have to

01:16:56.411 --> 01:16:57.590
do some work with

01:16:57.591 --> 01:16:59.905
the operating system
to try and fix that.

01:16:59.906 --> 01:17:04.400
So, let me close by just
briefly telling you about what

01:17:04.401 --> 01:17:06.930
the challenges are in

01:17:06.931 --> 01:17:10.344
building secure enclaves
in speculative processors.

01:17:10.345 --> 01:17:13.935
This material covered in a
couple of different ways.

01:17:13.936 --> 01:17:16.250
So, very briefly we

01:17:16.251 --> 01:17:18.655
want to shrink
the trusted computing base,

01:17:18.656 --> 01:17:24.285
you don't want all of the
operating system to be trusted.

01:17:24.286 --> 01:17:26.360
You'd like to minimize that

01:17:26.361 --> 01:17:29.150
to perhaps as security
monitor as is

01:17:29.151 --> 01:17:31.260
the case in Sanctum or

01:17:31.261 --> 01:17:34.220
microcode as is
the case in Intel SGX.

01:17:34.221 --> 01:17:38.105
SGX has some nice properties
associated with it,

01:17:38.106 --> 01:17:40.010
it does all of these things and

01:17:40.011 --> 01:17:41.890
shrinks the trusted
computing base but it

01:17:41.891 --> 01:17:46.000
has some serious issues
associated with side channels.

01:17:46.001 --> 01:17:48.600
It leaks privacy in
many different ways.

01:17:48.601 --> 01:17:50.510
Microsoft had
a very nice paper that was

01:17:50.511 --> 01:17:53.595
mentioned on a leak
through the page tables,

01:17:53.596 --> 01:17:55.530
through the page
fault mechanism but

01:17:55.531 --> 01:17:57.790
there's other ways and
hyperthreading for

01:17:57.791 --> 01:17:59.970
example is yet another way

01:17:59.971 --> 01:18:02.290
and we've already talked
about speculation.

01:18:02.291 --> 01:18:04.520
Unfortunately, all
of these things have

01:18:04.521 --> 01:18:06.520
gotten to the point
where now you can take

01:18:06.521 --> 01:18:10.800
SGX machines rumor has it since
I haven't read the paper,

01:18:10.801 --> 01:18:13.650
but it's going to come out
August 14th and you can

01:18:13.651 --> 01:18:17.095
extract private keys
from SGX machines

01:18:17.096 --> 01:18:20.500
when the machine is running
anonymous attestation using

01:18:20.501 --> 01:18:22.365
EPID because there was

01:18:22.366 --> 01:18:24.890
cats that I talked
about back in the RSA,

01:18:24.891 --> 01:18:27.269
example weren't
completely balanced

01:18:27.270 --> 01:18:29.480
and therefore that these
micro architectural channels,

01:18:29.481 --> 01:18:31.090
exfiltration through
the cache and

01:18:31.091 --> 01:18:33.735
also through out-of-order
execution so,

01:18:33.736 --> 01:18:35.940
a subset of the people who did

01:18:35.941 --> 01:18:38.250
the spectra attack published

01:18:38.251 --> 01:18:39.420
this paper that's
going to come out

01:18:39.421 --> 01:18:41.580
as I mentioned on
August 14th and using

01:18:41.581 --> 01:18:43.900
insecurity which are
essentially breaking

01:18:43.901 --> 01:18:45.460
the SGX ecosystem and I

01:18:45.461 --> 01:18:47.100
think the title of
the paper is something

01:18:47.101 --> 01:18:49.200
like Extracting The Keys of

01:18:49.201 --> 01:18:51.515
The Intel SGX Kingdom
or something like that.

01:18:51.516 --> 01:18:55.110
So, that's not very
satisfying obviously.

01:18:55.111 --> 01:18:57.050
So, there's work to be done.

01:18:57.051 --> 01:19:00.015
So, this wasn't
because we were smart,

01:19:00.016 --> 01:19:01.230
it was probably more
because we were

01:19:01.231 --> 01:19:03.330
lucky that we decided
that we would

01:19:03.331 --> 01:19:06.620
build non-speculative
processors way

01:19:06.621 --> 01:19:08.860
back when we started
our sanctum project.

01:19:08.861 --> 01:19:11.235
Speculation is hard
to build in hardware

01:19:11.236 --> 01:19:14.530
I knew about a whole bunch
of other attacks and I

01:19:14.531 --> 01:19:16.620
don't know of a way of isolating

01:19:16.621 --> 01:19:19.305
hyperthreaded applications
from each other so we decided

01:19:19.306 --> 01:19:21.540
we wouldn't do
hyperthreading either but we

01:19:21.541 --> 01:19:24.670
did definitely look into
the cache tag channels and we

01:19:24.671 --> 01:19:28.220
have a petition caches
and sanctum and we clean

01:19:28.221 --> 01:19:32.505
up after ourselves in terms
of the time shared processes.

01:19:32.506 --> 01:19:33.930
I want to say a little bit about

01:19:33.931 --> 01:19:35.120
Sanctum's Chain of Trust,

01:19:35.121 --> 01:19:36.465
Dawn mentioned this.

01:19:36.466 --> 01:19:38.780
So, the idea really here

01:19:38.781 --> 01:19:41.280
is that you build
processors that have

01:19:41.281 --> 01:19:43.850
again public keys and private
keys associated with them

01:19:43.851 --> 01:19:45.220
and the way it
works in sanctum is

01:19:45.221 --> 01:19:46.705
that no one one the private key.

01:19:46.706 --> 01:19:50.020
It's generated randomly when
that chip is fabricated,

01:19:50.021 --> 01:19:52.340
that chip is powered up
I should say or on an

01:19:52.341 --> 01:19:55.180
FPGA and privilege
doesn't imply trust,

01:19:55.181 --> 01:19:57.065
but you do have machine mode

01:19:57.066 --> 01:19:59.670
where the small piece of code
called the security monitor

01:19:59.671 --> 01:20:02.370
runs and it essentially
verifies all of

01:20:02.371 --> 01:20:05.405
the allocation decisions that
the operating system makes,

01:20:05.406 --> 01:20:08.005
and you can get
secure enclaves using

01:20:08.006 --> 01:20:09.840
this mechanism and of

01:20:09.841 --> 01:20:12.380
course the key question is
what's your threat model?

01:20:12.381 --> 01:20:15.860
Did you incorporate
all of the attacks

01:20:15.861 --> 01:20:17.320
that are possible today in

01:20:17.321 --> 01:20:19.395
the threat model and did
you design for that?

01:20:19.396 --> 01:20:21.815
And it's simply armor ammunition,

01:20:21.816 --> 01:20:23.645
it's going to keep going,

01:20:23.646 --> 01:20:26.130
I don't see an end to that.

01:20:26.131 --> 01:20:27.615
Again, Dawn mention this.

01:20:27.616 --> 01:20:30.360
The page fault attack
is an important attack,

01:20:30.361 --> 01:20:32.540
it's I would say
a design flaw on SGX.

01:20:32.541 --> 01:20:34.190
We fixed it a couple of

01:20:34.191 --> 01:20:37.180
different ways in
Sanctum verses Keystone.

01:20:37.181 --> 01:20:41.130
In Sanctum, there's
two sets of page tables,

01:20:41.131 --> 01:20:43.885
the enclave page tables are
inside of enclave memory.

01:20:43.886 --> 01:20:46.730
So, that fixes
this page fault attack

01:20:46.731 --> 01:20:49.085
in the sense that you
can't see those addresses

01:20:49.086 --> 01:20:50.665
but there's other ways
of doing it

01:20:50.666 --> 01:20:51.890
including as Dawn mentioned

01:20:51.891 --> 01:20:53.280
using the physical
memory protection

01:20:53.281 --> 01:20:55.215
primitive in risk five.

01:20:55.216 --> 01:20:57.120
So, just to give you a sense of

01:20:57.121 --> 01:20:59.270
what it takes to
build an enclave.

01:20:59.271 --> 01:21:03.595
I've shown you this picture
obviously not possible here,

01:21:03.596 --> 01:21:06.115
but the colors are what
you want to look at,

01:21:06.116 --> 01:21:09.390
those are the things that
changed, really the interfaces,

01:21:09.391 --> 01:21:11.030
we needed reset logic for all of

01:21:11.031 --> 01:21:12.940
the orange colors and
then we needed to

01:21:12.941 --> 01:21:15.190
add a little bit of logic
corresponding to the blues.

01:21:15.191 --> 01:21:16.970
So, it's about
a two 2 percent change.

01:21:16.971 --> 01:21:18.750
This is what I meant
by it's not invasive,

01:21:18.751 --> 01:21:20.045
at least from my standpoint,

01:21:20.046 --> 01:21:22.110
in terms of actually
taking a processor or

01:21:22.111 --> 01:21:24.950
a stock processor and
putting enclaves on it.

