01:26:23.101 --> 01:26:24.420
&gt;&gt; All right. All
right. Thank you very

01:26:24.421 --> 01:26:26.730
much for the introduction, Manel.

01:26:26.731 --> 01:26:29.140
So, what I would
like to talk about

01:26:29.141 --> 01:26:31.310
today is some work that
we've been doing over

01:26:31.311 --> 01:26:34.650
the last couple of years
on thinking how to provide

01:26:34.651 --> 01:26:37.430
system support for
Trusted Execution

01:26:37.431 --> 01:26:39.140
in a Cloud environment.

01:26:39.141 --> 01:26:41.420
So, what got us

01:26:41.421 --> 01:26:44.200
excited about the
Trusted Execution Model,

01:26:44.201 --> 01:26:46.030
that it essentially, well,

01:26:46.031 --> 01:26:48.850
deals with the problem that
the tenant can move to

01:26:48.851 --> 01:26:51.030
a Cloud environment
without having to

01:26:51.031 --> 01:26:53.560
implicitly trust
that Cloud provider.

01:26:53.561 --> 01:26:55.720
But the question here becomes,

01:26:55.721 --> 01:26:59.060
what system support
needs to exist in

01:26:59.061 --> 01:27:00.880
that Cloud environment
so that you

01:27:00.881 --> 01:27:03.565
can actually move
existing workloads?

01:27:03.566 --> 01:27:07.630
So, the issue is that
a Cloud tenant would

01:27:07.631 --> 01:27:10.260
effectively think of
the Cloud environment

01:27:10.261 --> 01:27:11.850
as an untrusted black box.

01:27:11.851 --> 01:27:15.210
So, there's a very large
and complex stack that

01:27:15.211 --> 01:27:19.080
the Cloud provider runs with
millions of lines of code,

01:27:19.081 --> 01:27:21.080
and obviously this is where

01:27:21.081 --> 01:27:25.475
a large number of
security issues can come from.

01:27:25.476 --> 01:27:28.860
So, when you have vulnerabilities
in the Hypervisor,

01:27:28.861 --> 01:27:30.590
in the OS kernel, and so on,

01:27:30.591 --> 01:27:33.695
they can be exploited with
large range of attacks.

01:27:33.696 --> 01:27:35.595
Then, similarly,
there's the issue of

01:27:35.596 --> 01:27:38.285
insider attacks where
people who have privileged

01:27:38.286 --> 01:27:40.040
access to the Cloud environment

01:27:40.041 --> 01:27:44.155
themselves can then compromise
the tenant applications.

01:27:44.156 --> 01:27:45.645
Actually, when you think about

01:27:45.646 --> 01:27:50.780
existing hardware security
mechanisms that we have,

01:27:50.781 --> 01:27:52.060
they don't really trying to

01:27:52.061 --> 01:27:54.665
address this problem directly.

01:27:54.666 --> 01:27:56.860
Because the regular model is that

01:27:56.861 --> 01:27:58.080
the Cloud provider actually

01:27:58.081 --> 01:27:59.630
doesn't trust the Cloud tenant.

01:27:59.631 --> 01:28:02.360
So, a lot of the isolation
mechanisms that we have,

01:28:02.361 --> 01:28:05.150
they're all about protecting
the privileged part of

01:28:05.151 --> 01:28:08.740
the Cloud infrastructure
from tenant applications.

01:28:08.741 --> 01:28:11.305
But this is essentially
what the Cloud tenant

01:28:11.306 --> 01:28:14.080
wants is the reverse.

01:28:14.081 --> 01:28:16.140
So, this is what we thought is

01:28:16.141 --> 01:28:18.350
exciting about Trusted Execution,

01:28:18.351 --> 01:28:20.280
that it's a dedicated
security feature

01:28:20.281 --> 01:28:22.175
that actually gives control

01:28:22.176 --> 01:28:24.120
to Cloud tenants to protect

01:28:24.121 --> 01:28:27.100
their applications
and their data.

01:28:27.250 --> 01:28:29.700
So, one way to see this,

01:28:29.701 --> 01:28:30.840
when you look at SGX as

01:28:30.841 --> 01:28:32.820
an implementation of
Trusted Execution,

01:28:32.821 --> 01:28:34.795
it's actually a
user space concept.

01:28:34.796 --> 01:28:37.470
So, you don't need to
be a privileged process

01:28:37.471 --> 01:28:40.490
if you want to create an enclave,

01:28:40.491 --> 01:28:41.660
and that enclave memories

01:28:41.661 --> 01:28:44.075
then encrypted and
integrity-protected,

01:28:44.076 --> 01:28:49.030
and only the protected code
can access that memory,

01:28:49.031 --> 01:28:52.230
but the hardware will
prevent access from

01:28:52.231 --> 01:28:54.720
higher privileged parts
of the environment

01:28:54.721 --> 01:28:57.670
such as the OS kernel
or the hypervisor.

01:28:57.671 --> 01:29:00.505
So, Cloud tenant can directly,

01:29:00.506 --> 01:29:03.535
ideally, take advantage
of this hardware feature.

01:29:03.536 --> 01:29:05.000
But the question becomes,

01:29:05.001 --> 01:29:07.030
well, so if you are
a Cloud tenant,

01:29:07.031 --> 01:29:09.150
you want to move
a particular workload

01:29:09.151 --> 01:29:11.339
to a Cloud with
Trusted Execution,

01:29:11.340 --> 01:29:13.810
you want to take advantage
of this model, well,

01:29:13.811 --> 01:29:15.870
how can you do this without

01:29:15.871 --> 01:29:18.215
modifications to
your application?

01:29:18.216 --> 01:29:20.550
So, the question here is

01:29:20.551 --> 01:29:23.400
what is the support
that should exist in

01:29:23.401 --> 01:29:25.600
that Cloud environment so
that people can actually

01:29:25.601 --> 01:29:30.180
use Trusted Execution
or Intel SGX.

01:29:30.181 --> 01:29:32.415
So, what we've been doing is

01:29:32.416 --> 01:29:34.560
exploring this design space of

01:29:34.561 --> 01:29:36.790
different types of
systems support that

01:29:36.791 --> 01:29:39.225
we could offer to Cloud tenants.

01:29:39.226 --> 01:29:43.165
They're a couple of
things that one can do.

01:29:43.166 --> 01:29:44.870
So, probably one of

01:29:44.871 --> 01:29:47.290
the earliest approaches
that was proposed,

01:29:47.291 --> 01:29:49.575
I would call the
Library OS approached,

01:29:49.576 --> 01:29:51.950
so this is represented by work

01:29:51.951 --> 01:29:54.515
that Microsoft did on
a system called Haven,

01:29:54.516 --> 01:29:56.385
where you effectively take

01:29:56.386 --> 01:29:59.785
an application with all
of its dependencies,

01:29:59.786 --> 01:30:02.120
and an entire
library operating system,

01:30:02.121 --> 01:30:05.375
and you place this inside
of an SGX enclave.

01:30:05.376 --> 01:30:08.730
So, then you have effectively
a self-contained way of

01:30:08.731 --> 01:30:12.225
supporting that application
inside of the enclave.

01:30:12.226 --> 01:30:15.295
Then you're only exposing
a very thin Hypercall like

01:30:15.296 --> 01:30:18.970
Interface to the outside
to do, well,

01:30:18.971 --> 01:30:22.250
some low-level IO
operations where you rely

01:30:22.251 --> 01:30:25.920
on the outside environment.

01:30:25.921 --> 01:30:28.250
So, when you think
about this model,

01:30:28.251 --> 01:30:32.590
it has a very clearly defined
interface to the outside.

01:30:32.591 --> 01:30:33.650
But you potentially have

01:30:33.651 --> 01:30:35.670
a large trusted computing base

01:30:35.671 --> 01:30:37.950
because of the library
operating system in all of

01:30:37.951 --> 01:30:39.910
the systems support that you're

01:30:39.911 --> 01:30:43.875
putting inside of the enclave.

01:30:43.876 --> 01:30:47.030
So, we've been exploring
that model with a system called

01:30:47.031 --> 01:30:50.470
SGX-LKL that I'm going to
talk about more in a moment,

01:30:50.471 --> 01:30:53.850
that is a library
OS for supporting

01:30:53.851 --> 01:30:58.595
unmodified Linux binaries
that execute an SGX enclaves.

01:30:58.596 --> 01:31:03.880
So, another way of designing
system support is to take

01:31:03.881 --> 01:31:06.350
a slightly different approach
where you reduce

01:31:06.351 --> 01:31:08.090
the trusted computing base

01:31:08.091 --> 01:31:09.870
by only placing the application,

01:31:09.871 --> 01:31:11.800
its library dependencies, and

01:31:11.801 --> 01:31:15.560
the standard C library
inside of an SGX enclave.

01:31:15.561 --> 01:31:17.530
So, this is something
that we've explored in

01:31:17.531 --> 01:31:19.745
a system called SCONE,

01:31:19.746 --> 01:31:21.830
that does this for supporting

01:31:21.831 --> 01:31:24.855
Linux applications
inside of SGX enclaves.

01:31:24.856 --> 01:31:27.160
So, now the interface
that you're exposing to

01:31:27.161 --> 01:31:28.550
the outside world is essentially

01:31:28.551 --> 01:31:29.910
the system call interface.

01:31:29.911 --> 01:31:31.600
So, it's a much
broader interface than

01:31:31.601 --> 01:31:34.080
more calls that are exposed,

01:31:34.081 --> 01:31:36.815
but you've reduced
the trusted computing base.

01:31:36.816 --> 01:31:40.180
Because inside of your enclave,

01:31:40.181 --> 01:31:41.760
you only have
a standard C library,

01:31:41.761 --> 01:31:45.040
but you no longer have the
library operating system.

01:31:45.041 --> 01:31:47.190
Then finally, you can think

01:31:47.191 --> 01:31:49.500
of partitioning
your application as

01:31:49.501 --> 01:31:51.660
the furthest that you can go in

01:31:51.661 --> 01:31:54.590
terms of reducing
the trusted computing base.

01:31:54.591 --> 01:31:57.455
So, here what we're doing is
we're take an application.

01:31:57.456 --> 01:32:00.070
We're only taking
the security sensitive functions

01:32:00.071 --> 01:32:01.130
of that application.

01:32:01.131 --> 01:32:05.010
This is what we're placing
inside of the SGX enclave.

01:32:05.011 --> 01:32:08.220
Then we have an application
specific interface to

01:32:08.221 --> 01:32:11.419
the outside world
of function calls,

01:32:11.420 --> 01:32:14.890
so that the security
sensitive functions can

01:32:14.891 --> 01:32:16.580
interact with the rest

01:32:16.581 --> 01:32:18.595
of the application
that runs outside.

01:32:18.596 --> 01:32:23.855
So, this gives us
a small trusted computing base,

01:32:23.856 --> 01:32:25.910
but it does also incur

01:32:25.911 --> 01:32:29.115
a more complex interface
to the outside world.

01:32:29.116 --> 01:32:31.870
So, the SDK that Intel

01:32:31.871 --> 01:32:33.160
released for creating SGX

01:32:33.161 --> 01:32:34.770
applications follows that model.

01:32:34.771 --> 01:32:37.445
It essentially gives you
an RPC-like mechanism

01:32:37.446 --> 01:32:42.230
for placing some functions
inside of an enclave.

01:32:42.231 --> 01:32:44.380
So, piece of work
that we've done is

01:32:44.381 --> 01:32:46.180
a framework called Glamdring,

01:32:46.181 --> 01:32:48.500
that I'll also talk
a little bit about,

01:32:48.501 --> 01:32:52.065
that tries to help developers
making the decision

01:32:52.066 --> 01:32:54.180
which functions from
the application

01:32:54.181 --> 01:32:56.380
to place inside of
an SGX enclave,

01:32:56.381 --> 01:32:59.455
and what to execute outside.

01:32:59.456 --> 01:33:02.090
Okay, so let's talk
a little bit more

01:33:02.091 --> 01:33:04.365
about the Library OS approach.

01:33:04.366 --> 01:33:06.550
So, what are some of
the challenges with that?

01:33:06.551 --> 01:33:10.650
So, one significant challenge
is performance.

01:33:10.651 --> 01:33:12.840
Because enclave transitions
by themselves,

01:33:12.841 --> 01:33:15.690
they're expensive because, well,

01:33:15.691 --> 01:33:17.800
the hardware has to
perform particular checks

01:33:17.801 --> 01:33:22.640
whenever execution transitions
in and out of an enclave.

01:33:22.641 --> 01:33:25.260
Since we have to exit

01:33:25.261 --> 01:33:27.720
an enclave whenever we want
to invoke a system call,

01:33:27.721 --> 01:33:29.100
we will naturally incur

01:33:29.101 --> 01:33:31.230
those expensive transitions when

01:33:31.231 --> 01:33:33.105
we run a particular workload.

01:33:33.106 --> 01:33:35.920
So, this performance overhead
can be quite significant.

01:33:35.921 --> 01:33:37.560
So, this is an
experiment that we did

01:33:37.561 --> 01:33:39.700
where we measured the rate at

01:33:39.701 --> 01:33:41.700
which we can execute system calls

01:33:41.701 --> 01:33:43.890
when we increase
the number of threads,

01:33:43.891 --> 01:33:47.185
comparing native
execution with SGX.

01:33:47.186 --> 01:33:48.710
You can see that
you're losing about

01:33:48.711 --> 01:33:50.310
an order of magnitude

01:33:50.311 --> 01:33:53.630
in terms of performance
when you have SGX enclaves.

01:33:53.631 --> 01:33:56.240
So, for a system call
heavy workload,

01:33:56.241 --> 01:33:58.320
this is a significant problem.

01:33:58.321 --> 01:34:00.110
So, what can we do about this?

01:34:00.111 --> 01:34:02.870
Well, one obvious idea is

01:34:02.871 --> 01:34:05.980
simply to reduce this number
of enclave transitions.

01:34:05.981 --> 01:34:09.450
So, try to avoid
exiting an enclave and

01:34:09.451 --> 01:34:13.210
instead have threads stay
inside of the enclave.

01:34:13.211 --> 01:34:15.460
So, the more we can stay inside,

01:34:15.461 --> 01:34:18.455
the less we suffer from
that performance cost.

01:34:18.456 --> 01:34:19.920
So, how can we do this?

01:34:19.921 --> 01:34:21.250
Well, one thing that we've done,

01:34:21.251 --> 01:34:25.245
we used a level threading
inside of SGX enclaves.

01:34:25.246 --> 01:34:27.500
So, instead of relying

01:34:27.501 --> 01:34:29.955
on hardware threads
and preemption,

01:34:29.956 --> 01:34:31.695
we have used a level threading,

01:34:31.696 --> 01:34:35.370
and those threads essentially
never exit the enclave.

01:34:35.371 --> 01:34:37.350
Instead, we have
a scheduler inside of

01:34:37.351 --> 01:34:39.720
the enclave that switches
between those threads.

01:34:39.721 --> 01:34:43.180
That results in a significant
performance benefit.

01:34:43.181 --> 01:34:44.900
The other thing is to move

01:34:44.901 --> 01:34:49.100
some OS functionality
inside of the enclave.

01:34:49.101 --> 01:34:51.800
So, thread synchronization
is one example,

01:34:51.801 --> 01:34:55.510
memory management, file system,

01:34:55.511 --> 01:34:56.780
signal handling, and so on.

01:34:56.781 --> 01:34:59.680
So, things that normally
would invoke a system call

01:34:59.681 --> 01:35:03.520
the interaction with
the outside operating system.

01:35:03.521 --> 01:35:06.115
If you can move this inside
of the enclave,

01:35:06.116 --> 01:35:10.220
you avoid those expensive exits.

01:35:10.580 --> 01:35:12.980
So, these are
the ideas that we've

01:35:12.981 --> 01:35:16.190
then realized as part of SGX-LKL,

01:35:16.191 --> 01:35:18.930
that provides system
support for enclave.

01:35:18.931 --> 01:35:23.380
So, SGX-LKL can run unmodified
Linux applications.

01:35:23.381 --> 01:35:24.960
So, you take an application,

01:35:24.961 --> 01:35:27.120
all of its dependencies,

01:35:27.121 --> 01:35:29.845
you provide us an encrypted
file system image,

01:35:29.846 --> 01:35:33.415
and you can then launch
it inside of an enclave.

01:35:33.416 --> 01:35:37.810
So, the way SGX-LKL provides
a library operating system,

01:35:37.811 --> 01:35:41.160
that it actually uses the
Linux Kernel as a library.

01:35:41.161 --> 01:35:42.960
So, it uses space on something

01:35:42.961 --> 01:35:45.235
called LKL then external library,

01:35:45.236 --> 01:35:47.710
which is an architecture
specific port

01:35:47.711 --> 01:35:49.480
of the Linux Kernel,

01:35:49.481 --> 01:35:51.290
and we can run this entirely in

01:35:51.291 --> 01:35:53.690
user space inside of an enclave.

01:35:53.691 --> 01:35:55.250
So, by doing this,
we for example get

01:35:55.251 --> 01:35:58.350
a trustworthy file system
and a network stack,

01:35:58.351 --> 01:36:03.255
that we can then exploit for SGX.

01:36:03.256 --> 01:36:04.710
So, there are a couple of

01:36:04.711 --> 01:36:07.970
interesting features
in the SGX-LKL design.

01:36:07.971 --> 01:36:10.795
So, one is that user
level threading.

01:36:10.796 --> 01:36:13.130
So, we can do
thread synchronization

01:36:13.131 --> 01:36:15.840
within enclaves without exiting.

01:36:15.841 --> 01:36:17.735
So, if a futex implementation

01:36:17.736 --> 01:36:22.805
inside of an enclave that
we can use for this.

01:36:22.806 --> 01:36:24.240
So, the other thing that we do,

01:36:24.241 --> 01:36:26.985
we support asynchronous
system calls.

01:36:26.986 --> 01:36:28.570
So, whenever the
application actually

01:36:28.571 --> 01:36:30.090
needs to execute a system call,

01:36:30.091 --> 01:36:32.000
because we have used
a level threading,

01:36:32.001 --> 01:36:35.580
we can ensure that the system

01:36:35.581 --> 01:36:37.200
call requests to simply put into

01:36:37.201 --> 01:36:39.365
a queue that is shared
with the outside,

01:36:39.366 --> 01:36:42.110
and we can show you
a different threat

01:36:42.111 --> 01:36:45.045
while we're waiting for
the result of that system call,

01:36:45.046 --> 01:36:47.080
and that can also
improve performance,

01:36:47.081 --> 01:36:49.140
and this is a technique
that we've also

01:36:49.141 --> 01:36:52.030
explored in the work on SCONE.

01:36:52.031 --> 01:36:54.430
Then finally, we have
a custom memory allocator,

01:36:54.431 --> 01:36:57.660
where we essentially do
our own memory management,

01:36:57.661 --> 01:37:01.515
both for the Kernel and
the enclave application inside.

01:37:01.516 --> 01:37:05.280
So, if one picture on
the SGX-LKL architecture,

01:37:05.281 --> 01:37:07.180
so this is what an enclave looks

01:37:07.181 --> 01:37:09.430
like when we're running
an application.

01:37:09.431 --> 01:37:11.950
So, we have that encrypted
file system image

01:37:11.951 --> 01:37:14.890
with the application
that we want to run,

01:37:14.891 --> 01:37:19.130
and then we have the LKL
library inside of the enclave,

01:37:19.131 --> 01:37:22.465
and in addition also
standards on C library.

01:37:22.466 --> 01:37:25.105
So, when we are launching

01:37:25.106 --> 01:37:28.495
an application using
SGX-LKL and enclave,

01:37:28.496 --> 01:37:30.390
we will first create
that enclave,

01:37:30.391 --> 01:37:33.590
we will then load LKL we will

01:37:33.591 --> 01:37:37.215
boot the LKL Kernel
inside of the enclave,

01:37:37.216 --> 01:37:38.955
and then the Kernel will mount

01:37:38.956 --> 01:37:43.510
the encrypted disk image
with the application,

01:37:43.511 --> 01:37:46.440
and it will invoke
dynamic linker loader

01:37:46.441 --> 01:37:49.825
to the load the binary
into the enclave.

01:37:49.826 --> 01:37:52.515
So, when the application
then executes,

01:37:52.516 --> 01:37:58.400
it then can invoke functions
of the standard C library,

01:37:58.401 --> 01:38:00.010
and then whenever those functions

01:38:00.011 --> 01:38:01.330
need to run a system call,

01:38:01.331 --> 01:38:02.825
then this will then go,

01:38:02.826 --> 01:38:05.530
become a function
called in LKL and it

01:38:05.531 --> 01:38:08.280
will execute the
functionality within LKL.

01:38:08.281 --> 01:38:11.970
If then LKL needs to interact
with the actual hardware

01:38:11.971 --> 01:38:13.250
because for example
it needs to do

01:38:13.251 --> 01:38:15.775
an IO operation to
reach a disk block,

01:38:15.776 --> 01:38:18.420
or it needs to send or
receive a network packet,

01:38:18.421 --> 01:38:20.500
this is then done
through a set of

01:38:20.501 --> 01:38:23.575
shared cues that we have
with the outside world.

01:38:23.576 --> 01:38:27.025
Using our asynchronous
system call mechanism.

01:38:27.026 --> 01:38:30.555
So, this gives us a significant
performance improvement

01:38:30.556 --> 01:38:35.245
compared to a model where
we would exit the enclave.

01:38:35.246 --> 01:38:36.720
There are couple of other things

01:38:36.721 --> 01:38:37.950
that we had to worry about.

01:38:37.951 --> 01:38:40.210
So, another one is
signal handling.

01:38:40.211 --> 01:38:43.030
So, we need to have signals
support but the problem

01:38:43.031 --> 01:38:46.110
here is that whenever
the process receives a signal,

01:38:46.111 --> 01:38:48.060
it's actually delivered
to the outside,

01:38:48.061 --> 01:38:49.590
so what we do is register

01:38:49.591 --> 01:38:51.870
permissive signal handler
on the outside,

01:38:51.871 --> 01:38:55.530
and then demultiplex
the signals inside,

01:38:55.531 --> 01:38:56.730
so that we can deliver them to

01:38:56.731 --> 01:39:00.935
the correct user level threats
inside of the SGX enclave.

01:39:00.936 --> 01:39:04.130
So, with this model,
we can now provide

01:39:04.131 --> 01:39:08.280
a trusted Linux file system
inside of the SGX enclave.

01:39:08.281 --> 01:39:10.180
We can actually support
any file system that

01:39:10.181 --> 01:39:13.545
is included in the Linux Kernel.

01:39:13.546 --> 01:39:15.250
For the encryption and integrity

01:39:15.251 --> 01:39:16.690
protection of the file system,

01:39:16.691 --> 01:39:19.125
we can use standard
Linux facilities,

01:39:19.126 --> 01:39:22.130
like for example, device

01:39:22.131 --> 01:39:25.730
mapper API for encryption
and integrity protection.

01:39:25.731 --> 01:39:27.990
So similarly, for
the network stack

01:39:27.991 --> 01:39:29.150
we can do the same thing.

01:39:29.151 --> 01:39:32.210
So, we can support any network
protocol that is supported

01:39:32.211 --> 01:39:35.475
by the Linux Kernel,

01:39:35.476 --> 01:39:38.290
and then the actual encryption
of packets can happen

01:39:38.291 --> 01:39:41.560
at lower layer in
the network stack,

01:39:41.561 --> 01:39:45.839
so we can use layer two
or layer three encryption

01:39:45.840 --> 01:39:48.064
of the role packets.

01:39:48.065 --> 01:39:49.990
So, when we look at

01:39:49.991 --> 01:39:52.570
the interface that we're
exposing to the outside world,

01:39:52.571 --> 01:39:54.435
it's quite a thin interface.

01:39:54.436 --> 01:39:56.160
So, we can subdivide the calls

01:39:56.161 --> 01:39:58.800
into workload-independent
hosts calls.

01:39:58.801 --> 01:40:00.590
So, there are calls that actually

01:40:00.591 --> 01:40:02.380
will be called
during the bootup of

01:40:02.381 --> 01:40:04.360
LKL but they are independent of

01:40:04.361 --> 01:40:06.480
the application workload
that we're running,

01:40:06.481 --> 01:40:08.850
and then we have
some Workload-dependent calls,

01:40:08.851 --> 01:40:13.160
so mostly related to
block IO, and network IO.

01:40:13.161 --> 01:40:18.730
So, the reason why this
distinction is sensible is that,

01:40:18.731 --> 01:40:21.920
obviously, that host interface
itself is a side channel.

01:40:21.921 --> 01:40:23.290
So, we need to worry about

01:40:23.291 --> 01:40:26.540
protecting those workload
dependent hosts calls,

01:40:26.541 --> 01:40:29.395
and ensuring that they
don't leak any information

01:40:29.396 --> 01:40:32.430
about what is happening
inside of the enclave.

01:40:32.431 --> 01:40:34.610
This is for example
one piece of ongoing work

01:40:34.611 --> 01:40:36.900
that we're doing is that we
are asking the question,

01:40:36.901 --> 01:40:40.420
"Can we make this host interface
itself Oblivious?"

01:40:40.421 --> 01:40:43.400
So, can we make sure
that an attacker

01:40:43.401 --> 01:40:46.650
that will observe the entire
sequence of host calls,

01:40:46.651 --> 01:40:48.830
will not learn
anything about what

01:40:48.831 --> 01:40:52.200
is happening inside
of the SGX enclave.

01:40:52.320 --> 01:40:58.295
So, SGX-LKL is available
as open source on GitHub,

01:40:58.296 --> 01:41:00.260
so you can play around with it,

01:41:00.261 --> 01:41:04.625
you can launch Linux binaries
from an Alpine Linux image,

01:41:04.626 --> 01:41:06.490
and the other interesting
thing is that,

01:41:06.491 --> 01:41:08.780
we have support for
managed language runtime.

01:41:08.781 --> 01:41:10.700
So, you can use SGX-LKL to run

01:41:10.701 --> 01:41:14.575
the Java Virtual Machine or
the V8 JavaScript engine.

01:41:14.576 --> 01:41:17.385
So, you can effectively
run Java applications

01:41:17.386 --> 01:41:21.970
inside of SGX enclaves.

01:41:21.971 --> 01:41:25.730
Okay. So, this is
an example of using

01:41:25.731 --> 01:41:29.350
a library operating system to
support Cloud application.

01:41:29.351 --> 01:41:31.650
So, let's look at
the other extreme

01:41:31.651 --> 01:41:33.705
of partitioning and application.

01:41:33.706 --> 01:41:36.020
So, why is that
an interesting thing to do?

01:41:36.021 --> 01:41:38.120
Well, so what about
if you want to run

01:41:38.121 --> 01:41:39.820
a really large Cloud application

01:41:39.821 --> 01:41:41.680
inside of an SGX enclave.

01:41:41.681 --> 01:41:43.450
So, let's say we want to support

01:41:43.451 --> 01:41:46.145
Apache Spark inside
of an SGX enclave.

01:41:46.146 --> 01:41:48.795
So here, we're looking at
a large amount of code,

01:41:48.796 --> 01:41:51.160
if we place all of this inside of

01:41:51.161 --> 01:41:54.680
an enclave for example
using SGX-LKL,

01:41:54.681 --> 01:41:56.155
we potentially looking at

01:41:56.156 --> 01:41:59.115
many vulnerabilities that
would be in that code base.

01:41:59.116 --> 01:42:01.300
So, instead, what we would
like to do is simply

01:42:01.301 --> 01:42:03.940
identify the security
sensitive parts,

01:42:03.941 --> 01:42:05.300
which is the data that we're

01:42:05.301 --> 01:42:08.510
processing and job
implementation,

01:42:08.511 --> 01:42:10.390
the task implementation of

01:42:10.391 --> 01:42:13.350
the analytics job
that we're running.

01:42:13.351 --> 01:42:16.860
So, in the research community
the many examples where

01:42:16.861 --> 01:42:20.320
people have partitioned
applications for SGX manually.

01:42:20.321 --> 01:42:21.770
So, we've done some work on

01:42:21.771 --> 01:42:23.475
a system called "Secure Keeper",

01:42:23.476 --> 01:42:26.710
that partition Zookeeper into

01:42:27.570 --> 01:42:31.480
security sensitive parts
and then untrusted part,

01:42:31.481 --> 01:42:35.315
Microsoft did some work on
partitioning MapReduce.

01:42:35.316 --> 01:42:38.305
So, there are many examples
where people do this by hand,

01:42:38.306 --> 01:42:40.890
but what we were
interested in is,

01:42:40.891 --> 01:42:45.540
can we help developers to
ideally automatically determine

01:42:45.541 --> 01:42:47.900
the minimum functionality
that needs to be

01:42:47.901 --> 01:42:51.410
run inside of
an SGX enclave to achieve,

01:42:51.411 --> 01:42:54.900
to enforce a particular
security policy?

01:42:55.730 --> 01:43:00.130
&gt;&gt; So, we've created
a SGX partitioning framework

01:43:00.131 --> 01:43:01.555
called Glamdring,

01:43:01.556 --> 01:43:04.400
and it's a compiler based
framework for partitioning

01:43:04.401 --> 01:43:07.580
C applications at
the source code level.

01:43:07.581 --> 01:43:12.600
It works by executing
a number of steps.

01:43:12.601 --> 01:43:16.170
So, it relies on a developer
first annotating what

01:43:16.171 --> 01:43:18.060
the security sensitive parts

01:43:18.061 --> 01:43:19.410
are that should be protected.

01:43:19.411 --> 01:43:21.750
So, this is essentially
the security policy

01:43:21.751 --> 01:43:23.895
that a developer
needs to express.

01:43:23.896 --> 01:43:25.280
So, once they've done this,

01:43:25.281 --> 01:43:27.240
there's static analysis that

01:43:27.241 --> 01:43:29.170
we perform at
the source code level,

01:43:29.171 --> 01:43:31.660
to then understand which parts

01:43:31.661 --> 01:43:34.220
of that application that
need to be protected.

01:43:34.221 --> 01:43:37.950
Then we rely on source to
source transformation to

01:43:37.951 --> 01:43:40.070
create the enclave part

01:43:40.071 --> 01:43:42.650
of the application in
the untrusted part.

01:43:42.651 --> 01:43:45.725
So, let's briefly look at
this in a bit more detail.

01:43:45.726 --> 01:43:48.210
So, first, you need to provide
the annotation and this is

01:43:48.211 --> 01:43:50.945
simply a source level annotation.

01:43:50.946 --> 01:43:52.710
So, in the example
of Spark, let's say,

01:43:52.711 --> 01:43:55.310
you want to protect
the data that initially is

01:43:55.311 --> 01:43:58.160
encrypted and sits on HDFS.

01:43:58.161 --> 01:44:01.720
So, say, you would
provide an annotation in

01:44:01.721 --> 01:44:06.600
the Spark code before it's
being read by the framework.

01:44:06.601 --> 01:44:08.995
So, you can add a pragma
that says that,

01:44:08.996 --> 01:44:11.720
data is sensitive and
should be protected

01:44:11.721 --> 01:44:16.215
throughout the execution
of the application.

01:44:16.216 --> 01:44:19.140
So, the way we then
use those annotations,

01:44:19.141 --> 01:44:20.850
is that we use them as part of

01:44:20.851 --> 01:44:22.610
the static analysis to figure

01:44:22.611 --> 01:44:24.815
out how to partition
the application.

01:44:24.816 --> 01:44:27.260
So, we generate the program
dependence graph that

01:44:27.261 --> 01:44:30.580
captures all of
the data and control

01:44:30.581 --> 01:44:36.335
flow dependencies between
different parts of the program,

01:44:36.336 --> 01:44:38.365
and we start our analysis at

01:44:38.366 --> 01:44:42.180
the particular nodes that

01:44:42.181 --> 01:44:44.315
were annotated by the developer.

01:44:44.316 --> 01:44:46.430
And to ensure
data confidentiality,

01:44:46.431 --> 01:44:47.985
we're interested in
everything that is

01:44:47.986 --> 01:44:49.670
affected by sensitive data.

01:44:49.671 --> 01:44:52.130
So, we do a forward
data flow analysis

01:44:52.131 --> 01:44:55.050
to enforce integrity policy,

01:44:55.051 --> 01:44:56.660
we essentially do the reverse.

01:44:56.661 --> 01:44:58.320
So we do a backflip,

01:44:58.321 --> 01:45:01.370
backward data flow
analysis to understand

01:45:01.371 --> 01:45:03.320
everything that's may impact

01:45:03.321 --> 01:45:05.815
a particular result that
we want to protect.

01:45:05.816 --> 01:45:09.275
So then, what we need to
place inside of the enclave,

01:45:09.276 --> 01:45:12.980
is essentially the union
of that analysis,

01:45:12.981 --> 01:45:14.830
and this is then what we

01:45:14.831 --> 01:45:18.525
consider to be part of
our trusted computing base.

01:45:18.526 --> 01:45:21.290
So, we then automatically rewrite

01:45:21.291 --> 01:45:23.660
the source code to implement
that partitioning,

01:45:23.661 --> 01:45:28.835
and we do this using
the LLVM compiler tool chain.

01:45:28.836 --> 01:45:31.620
By then rewriting the
application and essentially

01:45:31.621 --> 01:45:34.675
adding the appropriate enclave
and outside calls,

01:45:34.676 --> 01:45:37.020
so that we can do
the transitions when

01:45:37.021 --> 01:45:40.815
we move control in and
out of an enclave.

01:45:40.816 --> 01:45:44.020
There's actually one last step
that we need to deal with,

01:45:44.021 --> 01:45:45.370
and this has something to

01:45:45.371 --> 01:45:46.890
do with the fact that we rely on

01:45:46.891 --> 01:45:50.070
static analysis for
this partitioning.

01:45:50.071 --> 01:45:52.960
So, static analysis obviously
makes assumptions about,

01:45:52.961 --> 01:45:54.760
for example, valid execution in

01:45:54.761 --> 01:45:56.960
the semantics of
the programming language.

01:45:56.961 --> 01:45:59.250
The problem is that,
an attacker has complete

01:45:59.251 --> 01:46:01.570
control over code
that lives outside.

01:46:01.571 --> 01:46:04.079
In particular, they could violate

01:46:04.080 --> 01:46:07.725
those invariants
the static analysis used.

01:46:07.726 --> 01:46:10.110
So, therefore, we need to
make sure that whenever

01:46:10.111 --> 01:46:13.150
control moves into the enclave,

01:46:13.151 --> 01:46:15.105
those invariants actually hold.

01:46:15.106 --> 01:46:16.800
And the way we achieve this is by

01:46:16.801 --> 01:46:18.780
adding runtime checks that

01:46:18.781 --> 01:46:23.050
simply verify that
the partitioning is still sound,

01:46:23.051 --> 01:46:26.595
based on the assumptions that
the static analysis made.

01:46:26.596 --> 01:46:29.320
So, I'll slide on some results.

01:46:29.321 --> 01:46:31.330
So how well does this work?

01:46:31.331 --> 01:46:32.350
Well, so we did

01:46:32.351 --> 01:46:35.410
an evaluation with
three applications Memcached,

01:46:35.411 --> 01:46:38.410
DigitalBitBox which
is a Bitcoin wallet,

01:46:38.411 --> 01:46:41.200
and LibreSSL, a TLS
implementation,

01:46:41.201 --> 01:46:45.350
and we applied Glamdring to
partition the application,

01:46:45.351 --> 01:46:49.435
and the outcome was that
between 22 to 40 percent

01:46:49.436 --> 01:46:51.090
of the application is then placed

01:46:51.091 --> 01:46:53.865
inside of the enclave
and the rest is outside.

01:46:53.866 --> 01:46:55.730
So, obviously, there's
no magic here,

01:46:55.731 --> 01:46:57.370
it depends ultimately on

01:46:57.371 --> 01:46:59.010
how your application
is structured,

01:46:59.011 --> 01:47:01.920
how powerful
your static analysis is,

01:47:01.921 --> 01:47:04.760
and this will then
determine how big

01:47:04.761 --> 01:47:07.295
the trusted computing
base will be.

01:47:07.296 --> 01:47:09.170
But it's definitely a way of

01:47:09.171 --> 01:47:11.470
helping a developer
to effectively obtain

01:47:11.471 --> 01:47:14.220
a principled way to decide

01:47:14.221 --> 01:47:16.140
which functionality
they want to protect

01:47:16.141 --> 01:47:19.290
using an SGX enclave.

01:47:19.590 --> 01:47:22.480
So, to summarize, so trusted

01:47:22.481 --> 01:47:25.680
Execution in our eyes has a lot

01:47:25.681 --> 01:47:28.100
of promise in terms of dealing

01:47:28.101 --> 01:47:30.925
with real-world cloud
security problems today.

01:47:30.926 --> 01:47:32.760
But at the same time,
it also creates

01:47:32.761 --> 01:47:35.815
new challenges in terms
of system support and

01:47:35.816 --> 01:47:38.080
also tool support to

01:47:38.081 --> 01:47:41.255
take and port applications
that we have,

01:47:41.256 --> 01:47:45.185
and secure them using
Trusted Execution.

01:47:45.186 --> 01:47:47.990
So, if tenants want to

01:47:47.991 --> 01:47:50.940
run unmodified
existing applications,

01:47:50.941 --> 01:47:52.470
so approaches that, for example,

01:47:52.471 --> 01:47:54.510
use library operating system,

01:47:54.511 --> 01:47:56.040
can help with doing this and

01:47:56.041 --> 01:47:58.230
effectively doing
it transparently,

01:47:58.231 --> 01:47:59.500
but at the same time,

01:47:59.501 --> 01:48:01.625
it is also important
to think but how to

01:48:01.626 --> 01:48:05.005
integrate appropriate tools with

01:48:05.006 --> 01:48:08.680
development environments

01:48:08.681 --> 01:48:10.610
for new types of applications and

01:48:10.611 --> 01:48:12.450
the work that we've done
with Glamdring with

01:48:12.451 --> 01:48:16.955
static analysis is one
example of achieving this.

01:48:16.956 --> 01:48:19.440
Okay, so this is all I
wanted to share and I'm

01:48:19.441 --> 01:48:21.705
very happy to take
any questions. Thank you.

01:48:21.706 --> 01:48:28.540
&gt;&gt; Thank you Peter. Have time
for a couple of questions.

01:48:28.950 --> 01:48:42.640
Yes.

01:48:42.641 --> 01:48:42.740
[inaudible]

01:48:42.741 --> 01:48:45.460
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, the thing
to keep in mind

01:48:45.461 --> 01:48:48.590
with Trusted Execution
Models like SGX,

01:48:48.591 --> 01:48:50.440
is that they don't really have,

01:48:50.441 --> 01:48:53.710
they don't focus on
availability right.

01:48:53.711 --> 01:48:55.230
So, they don't give
you any guarantees

01:48:55.231 --> 01:48:57.355
in terms of availability.

01:48:57.356 --> 01:48:59.910
So, an outside attacker
that, for example,

01:48:59.911 --> 01:49:02.340
controls the schedule or
the operating system can

01:49:02.341 --> 01:49:05.075
simply withdraw resources
from the enclave.

01:49:05.076 --> 01:49:08.860
The enclave relies on
the outside scheduler to run.

01:49:08.861 --> 01:49:10.270
So effectively, the enclave

01:49:10.271 --> 01:49:12.815
cannot launch
a denial-of-service attack

01:49:12.816 --> 01:49:15.999
because its scheduling relies

01:49:16.000 --> 01:49:17.770
on the outside operating system.

01:49:17.771 --> 01:49:20.540
But in general, in all
of those approaches,

01:49:20.541 --> 01:49:24.650
they can't defend against
denial of service attacks.

01:49:25.390 --> 01:49:29.030
&gt;&gt; One more question? Yeah.

01:49:29.031 --> 01:49:35.740
&gt;&gt; Did you say something
about the memory [inaudible]

01:49:35.741 --> 01:49:37.715
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Well, I mean,

01:49:37.716 --> 01:49:40.720
it's actually not as
bad as one would think,

01:49:40.721 --> 01:49:42.200
because obviously one
of the advantage of

01:49:42.201 --> 01:49:44.120
Linux is that it has
been optimized for

01:49:44.121 --> 01:49:46.040
embedded architectures
and essentially it can

01:49:46.041 --> 01:49:48.315
run in low memory footprint.

01:49:48.316 --> 01:49:51.120
So, if we run SGX-LKL,

01:49:51.121 --> 01:49:52.250
we can fit within

01:49:52.251 --> 01:49:55.290
current EPC sizes, actually
quite comfortably.

01:49:55.291 --> 01:49:59.080
So it fits, so it uses
less than 96 megabytes.

01:49:59.081 --> 01:50:01.040
For some of the
larger applications,

01:50:01.041 --> 01:50:02.820
we will run out of EPC memory,

01:50:02.821 --> 01:50:04.835
so we will use SGX paging

01:50:04.836 --> 01:50:06.320
and the other
performance very much

01:50:06.321 --> 01:50:08.020
depends on whether
your working set

01:50:08.021 --> 01:50:10.010
will fit within the EPC or not.

01:50:10.011 --> 01:50:12.205
And again, for some workloads
that's the case,

01:50:12.206 --> 01:50:13.810
obviously once you run

01:50:13.811 --> 01:50:16.700
complex Java applications
with a JVM, yeah,

01:50:16.701 --> 01:50:19.320
at some point with
the current SGX implementations

01:50:19.321 --> 01:50:20.445
and the memory limits,

01:50:20.446 --> 01:50:22.665
you will see lower performance.

01:50:22.666 --> 01:50:26.095
But there's no
fundamental reason why

01:50:26.096 --> 01:50:28.010
its future SGX implementations

01:50:28.011 --> 01:50:30.865
could not have larger EPC sizes.

01:50:30.866 --> 01:50:33.020
&gt;&gt; Right, thank you
very much. Let's thank

01:50:33.021 --> 01:50:34.050
all the speakers and this

01:50:34.051 --> 01:50:36.370
concludes this
session. Thank you.

